#' Generate owning Float64 open-interval uniforms from a fresh MT19937 midpoint stream.
#'
#' Each raw 32-bit engine word r produces exactly (double(r)+.5)/2^32, in logical
#' row-major order. There is one engine draw per element, without a distribution
#' adapter, cached spare, clipping or shared generator. Values lie in
#' \[2^-33,1-2^-33\], with 32 bits of discrete resolution, and retain that strict
#' open interval in Float64. Casting to Float32 can round the upper end to1;
#' keep Float64 for APIs requiring open-interval categorical uniforms. This is
#' the source sentence-generation stream, distinct from nn::uniform()'s 53-bit
#' generator. Input shape is unchanged, output detached and independently owned.
#' Empty axes produce empty tensors; an empty shape produces one scalar.
#' Separate calls are thread-independent. Work/storage O(product(shape)); shape
#' and representable element/byte counts are checked before allocation.
#' @usage
#' MidpointUniformNoise(shape, seed)
#' @param shape `vector<integer>`. Nonnegative output dimensions; empty selects a scalar.
#' @param seed `integer`. Full unsigned32 local MT19937 seed, with no default.
#' @return `Tensor`: Contiguous detached Float64 tensor of exactly shape.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' const auto u=dm::nn::models::MidpointUniformNoise({20,7},3217);
#' // Explicit Float64[B,7] token uniforms; repeating the call repeats every bit.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For negative dimensions.
#' * `std::overflow_error`: For unrepresentable element, stride or byte counts.
#' @references - Matsumoto and Nishimura (1998), Mersenne Twister: A 623-dimensionally
#'   equidistributed uniform pseudorandom number generator,
#'   https://doi.org/10.1145/272991.272995 . The midpoint conversion follows the
#'   migrated teaching experiment, rather than a standard-library distribution.
#' @seealso GaussianNoise(), uniform(), SequenceGenerate()
#' @md
#'
`MidpointUniformNoise` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_348`, NULL, list(...))
#' Generate explicit Float64 Gaussian banks with the original MT19937/Box-Muller stream.
#'
#' A fresh std::mt19937(seed) supplies pairs u=(raw+.5)/2^32, v=(raw+.5)/2^32.
#' Successive values are sqrt(-2*log(u))*cos(2*pi*v), then the sine partner.
#' An odd final count discards the second partner. No standard-library normal
#' distribution, shared generator or cached spare is used. This preserves the
#' autoencoder examples' stream, distinct from nn::normal() and Mt19937::normal64().
#' Values are detached and newly owned, with a leading sample axis. Empty
#' dimensions/counts are supported; an empty sample shape produces \[samples\].
#' Work/storage O(samples*product(shape)); separate calls are thread-independent.
#' Transcendental rounding can vary between platforms/libm implementations.
#' @usage
#' GaussianNoise(shape, samples, seed)
#' @param shape `vector<integer>`. Shape of each bank entry; every dimension must be nonnegative.
#' @param samples `integer`. Nonnegative leading bank count.
#' @param seed `integer`. Raw 32-bit Mersenne Twister seed, with no default.
#' @return `Tensor`: Contiguous detached Float64 tensor \[samples,shape...\].
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' const auto bank = dm::nn::models::GaussianNoise({2,3}, 1, 1711);
#' // bank is Float64 [1,2,3]; repeating the call repeats its values.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For negative dimensions or sample count.
#' * `std::overflow_error`: If the nonzero element count exceeds int64.
#' @references - Box and Muller (1958), A Note on the Generation of Random Normal Deviates,
#'   https://doi.org/10.1214/aoms/1177706645 .
#' @seealso CorruptForDenoising(), normal()
#' @md
#'
`GaussianNoise` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_349`, NULL, list(...))
#' Add a supplied Gaussian bank to clean unit-range pixels, round to Float32 and clamp.
#'
#' Computes clamp(float(double(clean)+double(sigma)*noise),0,1), preserving the
#' source examples' Float32 sigma and rounding order, including signed zero.
#' Values outside Float32's finite range saturate before narrowing to avoid an
#' out-of-range floating conversion. Clipping changes the Gaussian corruption
#' law, including point masses at zero and one. Input noise is explicit: this
#' function never samples or changes a generator. Strided inputs are accepted;
#' values are copied, never mutated. The result is detached augmentation data,
#' with no input/noise autograd history. Noisy observations are inputs to a
#' separately evaluated model; the unchanged clean image remains the target.
#' Work/storage O(clean.numel()); no mutable shared state is used.
#' @usage
#' CorruptForDenoising(clean, standard_noise, sigma)
#' @param clean `Tensor`. Float32 unit-range finite tensor of rank at least two; empty axes allowed.
#' @param standard_noise `Tensor`. Finite Float64 tensor with shape \[1,clean.shape()...\].
#' @param sigma `numeric`. Finite nonnegative Float32 noise deviation in original pixel units.
#' @return `Tensor`: Newly owned contiguous Float32 tensor with clean's shape, in \[0,1\].
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n = dm::nn;
#' const auto clean = n::tensor({0.,.2,.8,1.},{2,2});
#' const auto z = n::tensor({-2.,1.,-1.,2.},{1,2,2},n::DType::Float64);
#' const auto noisy = n::models::CorruptForDenoising(clean,z,.3f);
#' // noisy is approximately [[0,.5],[.5,1]]. Clean is unchanged.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For undefined/wrong-shape/wrong-dtype tensors,
#'   invalid pixel/noise values or a negative/nonfinite sigma.
#' @references - Vincent, Larochelle, Bengio and Manzagol (2008), Extracting and Composing
#'   Robust Features with Denoising Autoencoders, https://doi.org/10.1145/1390156.1390294 .
#'   This implementation uses additive clipped Gaussian corruption; it is not
#'   the paper's masking-noise benchmark or a claim of universal denoising.
#' @seealso GaussianNoise(), UndercompleteAutoencoder(), mse()
#' @md
#'
`CorruptForDenoising` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_350`, NULL, list(...))
#' Decode four-channel spatial codes by nearest repetition and two convolutions.
#'
#' Repeats each spatial value into a 2-by-2 block, applies a same-padded 3-by-3
#' 4-to-8 ReLU convolution, repeats again, then applies a same-padded 3-by-3
#' 8-to-1 sigmoid convolution. Creates/reuses decoder1/W,b (He uniform/zeros)
#' and decoder2/W,b (Glorot uniform/zeros): 369 independent scalars. No tied
#' filters, transposed convolution, fitting, clipping or input mutation.
#' Strided Float32/64 inputs retain gradients. Shared stores need external
#' synchronization; allocation/backend errors propagate from tensor operations.
#' @usage
#' ConvolutionalDecoder(parameters, code)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable Float32/64 store matching code's dtype.
#' @param code `Tensor`. Nonempty floating NHWC tensor \[N,H,W,4\], with positive H,W and
#'   representable fourfold spatial dimensions.
#' @return `ConvolutionalDecoderOutput`: Differentiable intermediate map and reconstructed image.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n = dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(1801);
#' const auto result = n::models::ConvolutionalDecoder(p, n::ones({1, 2, 3, 4}));
#' // result.reconstruction is [1,8,12,1]; p.count() is 369.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For wrong shape/dtype or incompatible stored leaves.
#' @references - Masci, Meier, Ciresan and Schmidhuber (2011), Stacked Convolutional
#'   Auto-Encoders for Hierarchical Feature Extraction,
#'   https://people.idsia.ch/~ciresan/data/icann2011.pdf . This repository's
#'   variant uses untied filters, average pooling and nearest repetition;
#'   it does not reproduce the paper's stacked pretraining experiments.
#' @seealso ConvolutionalAutoencoder(), upsample_nearest(), conv2d()
#' @md
#'
`ConvolutionalDecoder` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_351`, NULL, list(...))
#' Encode grayscale images into spatial codes and reconstruct with untied convolutions.
#'
#' Uses same-padded 3-by-3 convolutions 1-to-8 and 8-to-4, each with ReLU and
#' nonoverlapping 2-by-2 average pooling, then ConvolutionalDecoder(). The eight
#' parameter tensors encoder1/W,b, encoder2/W,b, decoder1/W,b, decoder2/W,b
#' contain 741 scalars, independent of image dimensions. ReLU layers initialize
#' with He uniform and zero bias; the output uses Glorot uniform and zero bias.
#' Input/code coordinates differ fourfold in count, without quantization or a
#' bit-rate claim. No skip path, fitting, loss, clipping or input mutation is
#' hidden here. The caller chooses the target and training loop. Finite pixel
#' values in \[0,1\] are conventional, but values are not range-checked. Supports
#' strided Float32/64 tensors and input/parameter gradients. Invalid input is
#' rejected before any parameter creation; incompatible existing leaves can
#' throw after earlier layers have created their parameters. Shared stores need
#' external synchronization. Arithmetic and activation storage scale as O(NHW).
#' @usage
#' ConvolutionalAutoencoder(parameters, image)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable floating store matching image's dtype.
#' @param image `Tensor`. Nonempty NHWC \[N,H,W,1\], H and W positive multiples of four.
#' @return `ConvolutionalAeOutput`: All differentiable encoder/decoder endpoints, including the spatial code.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n = dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(1801);
#' const auto x = n::full({2, 8, 12, 1}, .25);
#' const auto net = n::models::ConvolutionalAutoencoder(p, x);
#' const auto loss = n::mse(net.reconstruction, x);
#' // net.code is [2,2,3,4]; p.count() is 741. The caller owns Adam.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For wrong shape/dtype or incompatible stored leaves.
#' @references - Masci, Meier, Ciresan and Schmidhuber (2011), Stacked Convolutional
#'   Auto-Encoders for Hierarchical Feature Extraction,
#'   https://people.idsia.ch/~ciresan/data/icann2011.pdf . This small untied
#'   average-pooling/MSE variant is not the paper's architecture or benchmark.
#' @seealso ConvolutionalAeOutput, ConvolutionalDecoder(), mse()
#' @md
#'
`ConvolutionalAutoencoder` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_352`, NULL, list(...))
#' Encode through a strict ReLU bottleneck and decode with a sigmoid layer.
#'
#' Computes ReLU(input*W_e+b_e), then sigmoid(code*W_d+b_d), using independent
#' encoder/decoder weights. Creates or reuses encoder/W, encoder/b, decoder/W
#' and decoder/b with Glorot-uniform weights and zero biases. There are
#' 2*features*latent+latent+features scalars. Input is never clipped or mutated;
#' finite normalized pixels are the usual input, but no value range is imposed.
#' Work is O(N*features*latent). Empty batches and nonfloating/mismatched dtypes
#' are rejected before creating parameters. A shared store needs external
#' synchronization. This forward function performs no optimization.
#' @usage
#' UndercompleteAutoencoder(parameters, input, features = 784, latent = 32)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable Float32/64 store, with the same dtype as input.
#' @param input `Tensor`. Floating nonempty matrix \[N,features\].
#' @param features `integer`. Input/output width greater than one; defaults to 784.
#' @param latent `integer`. Positive bottleneck width strictly below features; defaults to 32.
#' @return `DenseAeOutput`: The differentiable code and reconstruction, retaining input/store gradients.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n = dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore parameters(17);
#' const auto input = n::full({3, 4}, .25);
#' const auto result = n::models::UndercompleteAutoencoder(parameters, input, 4, 2);
#' const auto objective = n::mse(result.reconstruction, input);
#' // The caller chooses the optimizer and owns the training loop.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid shape, widths, dtype or incompatible stored leaves.
#' @references - Goodfellow, Bengio and Courville (2016), Deep Learning, section 14.1,
#'   https://www.deeplearningbook.org/contents/autoencoders.html .
#'   This variant uses a ReLU encoder and sigmoid decoder rather than a linear PCA model.
#' @seealso DenseAeOutput, nn::dense(), nn::mse()
#' @md
#'
`UndercompleteAutoencoder` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_353`, NULL, list(...))
#' Decode signed codes through the untied undercomplete autoencoder's sigmoid layer.
#'
#' Creates/reuses decoder/W\[latent,features\] and decoder/b\[features\] only, using
#' ordinary Glorot-uniform/zero initialization. Default widths give two leaves /
#' 25,872 scalars. Existing full-model stores keep their encoder leaves unchanged.
#' Code-only inference needs no images or labels and performs no fitting,
#' clipping or optimizer update. Caller restores learned arrays before deployed
#' inference; a decoder initialized alone consumes a different RNG sequence from
#' the full autoencoder. Finite signed strided matching Float32/64 CPU codes
#' retain code/decoder first and higher derivatives; outputs own lifetime. All
#' input checks precede leaf creation; incompatible existing leaves may fail
#' later. Computed overflow is not repaired. Shared stores require external
#' synchronization. Work O(B*latent*features), activation storage O(B*features).
#' @usage
#' UndercompleteDecoder(parameters, code, features = 784, latent = 32)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable matching floating store, creating/reusing decoder leaves.
#' @param code `Tensor`. Finite signed code matrix\[B,latent\], B positive.
#' @param features `integer`. Output width>1, default784.
#' @param latent `integer`. Positive code width<features, default32.
#' @return `Tensor`: Owning sigmoid reconstructions\[B,features\], with code/decoder paths.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore parameters(1471);
#' const auto full=n::models::UndercompleteAutoencoder(parameters,n::ones({2,4}),4,2);
#' const auto decoded=n::models::UndercompleteDecoder(parameters,full.code,4,2);
#' // decoded matches full.reconstruction; only codes enter this second call.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid geometry/dtype/values/products or incompatible leaves.
#' * `std::logic_error`: For non-CPU code storage.
#' @references - Goodfellow, Bengio and Courville (2016), Deep Learning, section14.1,
#'   https://www.deeplearningbook.org/contents/autoencoders.html . The migrated
#'   teaching variant uses a ReLU encoder and an untied sigmoid decoder.
#' @seealso UndercompleteAutoencoder(), DenseAeOutput, LatentFlatten()
#' @md
#'
`UndercompleteDecoder` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_354`, NULL, list(...))
#' Compute the exact squared Frobenius norm of a sigmoid encoder's input Jacobian.
#'
#' For code=sigmoid(input*encoder_weight+bias), computes
#' sum_j(code_j*(1-code_j))^2*sum_i(encoder_weight_ij)^2 for each row.
#' Both paths through code and weights remain differentiable, including higher
#' derivatives. Inputs are not detached, clipped or mutated. Supports strided
#' tensors. Work O(D*K+N*K), auxiliary output/intermediate storage O(N*K+K);
#' the full \[N,K,D\] Jacobian is not materialized. Values are not range/finiteness
#' checked: callers must supply actual sigmoid activations for this interpretation;
#' nonfinite values propagate through tensor arithmetic. No shared mutable state.
#' @usage
#' SigmoidEncoderContraction(code, encoder_weight)
#' @param code `Tensor`. Float32/64 nonempty matrix \[N,K\] of sigmoid encoder activations.
#' @param encoder_weight `Tensor`. Same-dtype nonempty matrix \[D,K\], with input features on rows.
#' @return `ContractivePenalty`: Per-observation squared norms and their batch mean, retaining both gradient paths.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n = dm::nn;
#' auto w = n::ones({3, 2}).requires_grad_();
#' const auto code = n::sigmoid(n::matmul(n::zeros({4, 3}), w));
#' const auto penalty = n::models::SigmoidEncoderContraction(code, w);
#' const auto derivative = n::grad(penalty.mean, {w})[0];
#' // All six entries of derivative are 0.125.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For undefined/nonmatrix/empty inputs, mismatched K or dtype.
#' @references - Rifai, Vincent, Muller, Glorot and Bengio (2011), Contractive Auto-Encoders:
#'   Explicit Invariance During Feature Extraction, section 4,
#'   https://icml.cc/Conferences/2011/papers/455_icmlpaper.pdf .
#' @seealso ContractivePenalty, ContractiveAutoencoder()
#' @md
#'
`SigmoidEncoderContraction` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_355`, NULL, list(...))
#' Evaluate an untied sigmoid autoencoder with its exact encoder contraction penalty.
#'
#' Creates/reuses encoder/W, encoder/b, decoder/W and decoder/b with independent
#' Glorot-uniform weights and zero biases. Both layers use sigmoid; the objective
#' is mean(square(reconstruction-input)) + weight*penalty.mean. Derivatives flow
#' through code and encoder weights. Does not fit, detach or mutate the input.
#' There are 2*features*latent+latent+features learned scalars. Any positive latent
#' width is accepted, including overcomplete encoders. Float32/64 input/store
#' dtypes must agree. Undefined/empty inputs and invalid settings are rejected
#' before parameter creation. Input values are not range/finiteness checked.
#' Work O(N*features*latent), intermediate storage O(N*(features+latent)); no full
#' Jacobian allocation. A shared parameter store needs external synchronization.
#' @usage
#' ContractiveAutoencoder(parameters, input, weight, features = 784, latent = 32)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable same-dtype learned parameter store.
#' @param input `Tensor`. Floating nonempty matrix \[N,features\], conventionally pixels in \[0,1\].
#' @param weight `numeric`. Finite nonnegative penalty coefficient; zero keeps the diagnostic but removes its loss contribution.
#' @param features `integer`. Positive input/output width, default 784.
#' @param latent `integer`. Positive sigmoid code width, default 32.
#' @return `ContractiveOutput`: Forward code/reconstruction, MSE, exact penalty and total objective.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n = dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(1753);
#' const auto result = n::models::ContractiveAutoencoder(p, n::full({2, 4}, .25), .01, 4, 2);
#' const auto derivatives = n::grad(result.loss, p.parameters());
#' // The caller owns the optimizer and the minibatch loop.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid settings, shape, dtype or incompatible stored leaves.
#' @references - Rifai et al. (2011), https://icml.cc/Conferences/2011/papers/455_icmlpaper.pdf .
#'   Retains the migrated example's untied weights, MSE and 32-unit bottleneck;
#'   the paper's experiments use tied weights and cross-entropy reconstruction.
#' @seealso SigmoidEncoderContraction(), UndercompleteAutoencoder()
#' @md
#'
`ContractiveAutoencoder` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_356`, NULL, list(...))
#' Select largest signed activities per observation or lifetime winners per feature.
#'
#' For lifetime=false, each row keeps min(k,F) features of scores\[B,F\]. For
#' lifetime=true, each column keeps ceil(B/8) observations; k is ignored after
#' validation. Ranking uses signed values, not magnitude; equal values select
#' lower column/row indices respectively. Selected zero values still count as
#' selected slots. Empty axes and strided inputs are supported. Only finite
#' Float32/64 matrices are accepted, avoiding undefined NaN ranking semantics.
#' Returns detached binary values of the same dtype/shape, without changing
#' scores. Differentiating scores*mask passes gradients at winners only; this
#' does not differentiate the discrete ranking or define a derivative at ties.
#' Uses stable sorting: O(B*F*log(F)) per-row or O(F*B*log(B)) lifetime work and
#' O(B*F+max(B,F)) auxiliary storage. No mutable shared state is used.
#' @usage
#' CompetitiveMask(scores, lifetime, k = 8)
#' @param scores `Tensor`. Finite floating activation matrix \[B,F\]; it need not be detached.
#' @param lifetime `logical`. Whether to compete across observations in each feature.
#' @param k `integer`. Nonnegative requested per-row count, default 8; larger counts saturate at F.
#' @return `Tensor`: Newly owned contiguous binary selector \[B,F\], without autograd history.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n = dm::nn;
#' const auto scores = n::tensor({-4.,-1.,-2.,-3.},{1,4});
#' const auto mask = n::models::CompetitiveMask(scores,false,2);
#' // mask is [[0,1,1,0]]; winners are -1 and -2, not largest magnitudes.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For undefined/nonmatrix/nonfloating/nonfinite scores or k<0.
#' @references - Makhzani and Frey (2013/2014), k-Sparse Autoencoders, https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.5663 .
#' - Makhzani and Frey (2014/2015), Winner-Take-All Autoencoders,
#'   https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.2752 . This lifetime variant fixes retention at one eighth.
#' @seealso CompetitiveAutoencoder(), top_k()
#' @md
#'
`CompetitiveMask` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_357`, NULL, list(...))
#' Evaluate signed k-sparse or lifetime winner-take-all encoder/decoder paths without fitting.
#'
#' Creates/reuses encoder/W,b and decoder/W,b with independent Glorot-uniform
#' weights and zero biases. Parameter count is 2*features*latent+latent+features
#' (101200 by default). Per-row competition uses a linear encoder and preserves
#' its hard mask at deployment. Lifetime competition uses ReLU, keeps ceil(B/8)
#' examples per feature during training and bypasses the gate at deployment.
#' plain=true bypasses either competition but retains the corresponding encoder
#' activation, parameter layout and both result paths. No target, optimizer,
#' training loop or global training-mode switch is hidden here. Losses are the
#' caller's choice. Supports strided inputs, input/parameter gradients and higher
#' derivatives on fixed support. Discrete support changes/ties have no promised
#' derivative. Input is not clipped or mutated. Nonfinite raw activities cause
#' CompetitiveMask() to throw unless plain=true, where they propagate normally.
#' Invalid input geometry/dtype/options are rejected before parameter creation;
#' incompatible existing leaves/nonfinite activities may fail after creation.
#' Shared stores require external synchronization. Dense work is O(B*features*latent),
#' plus the selector's sorting cost; training/deployment share decoder leaves.
#' @usage
#' CompetitiveAutoencoder(parameters, input, lifetime, plain = FALSE, features = 784, latent = 64, k = 8)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable Float32/64 store matching input's dtype.
#' @param input `Tensor`. Nonempty floating matrix \[B,features\].
#' @param lifetime `logical`. Select lifetime ReLU competition rather than per-row linear competition.
#' @param plain `logical`. Use the matched ungated control, default false.
#' @param features `integer`. Positive input/output width, default 784.
#' @param latent `integer`. Positive code width, default 64; strict undercompleteness is not required.
#' @param k `integer`. Nonnegative per-row retention count, default 8; ignored by lifetime/plain paths.
#' @return `CompetitiveAeOutput`: Owning training/deployment endpoints with differentiable continuous paths.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n = dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(1931);
#' const auto x = n::full({2,3},.25);
#' const auto net = n::models::CompetitiveAutoencoder(p,x,false,false,3,4,2);
#' const auto loss = n::mse(net.training_reconstruction,x);
#' // The caller owns Adam; 31 learned scalars and two selected slots per row.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid input/options, incompatible stored leaves or nonfinite ranked activities.
#' @references - Makhzani and Frey (2013/2014), k-Sparse Autoencoders, https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.5663 .
#' - Makhzani and Frey (2014/2015), Winner-Take-All Autoencoders,
#'   https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.2752 . These small untied sigmoid/MSE models on raw
#'   pixels are educational variants, not the original decoder, scale or classification experiments.
#' @seealso CompetitiveAeOutput, CompetitiveMask(), mse()
#' @md
#'
`CompetitiveAutoencoder` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_358`, NULL, list(...))
#' Map a row-major 7-by-7 patch element to its flat 28-by-28 image index.
