#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid width or nonfloating dtype.
#' @references - He et al. (2021/2022), Masked Autoencoders Are Scalable Vision Learners,
#'   https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.06377 . This is the source example's small fixed-grid variant.
#' @seealso MaeEncode(), MaeDecode()
#' @md
#'
`MaePosition` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_362`, NULL, list(...))
#' Select four distinct visible patches by stable per-record unsigned hashes.
#'
#' Ranks the source's exact wrapping 32-bit mix of ID, epoch, seed and patch index;
#' hash ties prefer lower patch indices. Visible rows follow hash order, not
#' spatial order. This is deterministic selection, not a uniform-randomness claim.
#' No language/global RNG state is read or changed. Empty ID vectors are allowed.
#' @usage
#' MaeSelection(source, epoch = 0, seed = 1907, dtype = "Float32")
#' @param source `Tensor`. Int32/64 vector of source IDs in \[0,4294967295\].
#' @param epoch `integer`. Whole epoch index in \[0,4294967295\], default zero for evaluation.
#' @param seed `integer`. Explicit unsigned seed, default 1907; changing it changes the masks.
#' @param dtype `character (DType)`. Float32 by default, or Float64, matching the later image/store.
#' @return `Tensor`: Detached binary \[B,4,16\] selector with four distinct one-hot rows per record.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' const auto selection=n::models::MaeSelection(n::zeros({2},n::DType::Int32));
#' // Two identical source IDs yield identical [4,16] selectors.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid ID shape/dtype/range, epoch or output dtype.
#' @references - He et al. (2021/2022), https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.06377 . The source's
#'   hash-derived 75-percent masks preserve its experiment, rather than the paper's random sampler.
#' @seealso MaskedAutoencoder(), MaeLoss()
#' @md
#'
`MaeSelection` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_363`, NULL, list(...))
#' Apply a two-head pre-normalized residual transformer block with tanh-GELU MLP.
#'
#' LayerNorm epsilon is 1e-5. Bias-free Q/K/V/output attention projections use
#' scaled dot-product softmax, followed by a second pre-norm residual MLP with
#' widths width→2*width→width. Dense MLPs have biases; no dropout or causal mask.
#' Leaves use standard layer initializers and names name/ln1, attention, ln2,
#' ff1 and ff2. Inputs are not mutated; shared stores require synchronization.
#' @usage
#' MaeBlock(parameters, name, x, width)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable Float32/64 store matching x.
#' @param name `character`. Nonempty parameter prefix.
#' @param x `Tensor`. Finite floating \[B,T,width\], with positive B,T.
#' @param width `integer`. Positive even feature width matching x's last dimension.
#' @return `MaeBlockOutput`: Owning block output and per-head attention handles with gradients retained.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(17);
#' const auto block=n::models::MaeBlock(p,"block",n::ones({2,3,4}),4);
#' // output [2,3,4]; attention [2,2,3,3].
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid geometry/dtype/prefix or incompatible stored leaves.
#' @references - He et al. (2021/2022), https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.06377 . Width, depth and
#'   MLP ratio here retain the tiny source example, not a full ViT benchmark.
#' @seealso MaeEncode(), MaeDecode(), multi_head_attention()
#' @md
#'
`MaeBlock` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_364`, NULL, list(...))
#' Encode only four visible patches into normalized width-32 tokens.
#'
#' Gather precedes the learned 49→32 patch embedding, so finite hidden pixel
#' changes do not enter the encoder. Selected fixed positions, one MaeBlock and
#' final LayerNorm form the code. No target is supplied or scored. Invalid
#' images/selectors/dtypes fail before parameter creation; later backend or
#' incompatible-leaf failures can leave earlier parameters created. No fitting,
#' clipping or input mutation; shared stores require external synchronization.
#' @usage
#' MaeEncode(parameters, image, selector)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable floating store matching image and selector.
#' @param image `Tensor`. Finite Float32/64 \[B,28,28,1\], B>0.
#' @param selector `Tensor`. Matching detached \[B,4,16\], four distinct one-hot visible rows.
#' @return `MaskedAeOutput`: Owning encoder fields documented in MaskedAeOutput; decoder fields undefined.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(1877);
#' const auto s=n::models::MaeSelection(n::zeros({1},n::DType::Int32));
#' const auto encoded=n::models::MaeEncode(p,n::zeros({1,28,28,1}),s);
#' // encoded.visible [1,4,49]; encoded.code [1,4,32].
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid inputs or incompatible stored leaves.
#' @references - He et al. (2021/2022), https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.06377 .
#' @seealso MaeDecode(), MaskedAutoencoder(), MaeLoss()
#' @md
#'
`MaeEncode` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_365`, NULL, list(...))
#' Decode visible codes and patch locations using learned mask tokens, without an image.
#'
#' Projects 32→16, scatters the four visible tokens with selector transpose,
#' inserts one shared learned 16-value mask token at hidden locations, adds fixed
#' positions, and runs one width-16 block, final LayerNorm and 16→49 sigmoid head.
#' Targets and hidden image pixels are absent from the signature. No visible
#' pixels are copied into predictions; a composite display is the caller's choice.
#' Invalid input fails before decoder parameter creation. Retains code gradients;
#' inputs are not mutated and shared stores need external synchronization.
#' @usage
#' MaeDecode(parameters, code, selector)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable floating store matching code/selector.
#' @param code `Tensor`. Finite Float32/64 \[B,4,32\], B>0.
#' @param selector `Tensor`. Matching detached \[B,4,16\], four distinct one-hot rows.
#' @return `MaskedAeOutput`: Owning decoder fields documented in MaskedAeOutput; encoder-only fields undefined.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(1877);
#' const auto s=n::models::MaeSelection(n::zeros({1},n::DType::Int32));
#' const auto decoded=n::models::MaeDecode(p,n::zeros({1,4,32}),s);
#' // decoded.prediction [1,16,49]; no original image or target was supplied.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid inputs or incompatible stored leaves.
#' @references - He et al. (2021/2022), https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.06377 .
#' @seealso MaeEncode(), MaskedAutoencoder()
#' @md
#'
`MaeDecode` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_366`, NULL, list(...))
#' Reconstruct sixteen patches with the original tiny visible-only transformer MAE.
#'
#' Composes MaeEncode and MaeDecode without fitting or scoring. The fixed
#' width-32 encoder sees four 7-by-7 patches; the width-16 decoder sees sixteen
#' locations. No CLS token or dropout; 35 parameter tensors contain 13649 scalars.
#' Names preserve patch, encoder, decoder_projection, mask_token, decoder and
#' prediction prefixes. Standard layer initializers use independent weights;
#' LayerNorm has unit gamma/zero beta and the mask token starts at zero.
#' Targets are retained in patches only for caller-owned loss calculation.
#' All discrete selector checks precede parameter creation; numerical/later
#' backend failures may occur after partial creation. Inputs are not mutated;
#' shared stores require synchronization. Supports strided Float32/64 tensors.
#' @usage
#' MaskedAutoencoder(parameters, image, selector)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable floating store matching image/selector.
#' @param image `Tensor`. Finite nonempty grayscale \[B,28,28,1\].
#' @param selector `Tensor`. Detached matching \[B,4,16\] with four distinct one-hot rows.
#' @return `MaskedAeOutput`: All owning encoder/decoder endpoints with continuous gradients retained.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(1877);
#' const auto s=n::models::MaeSelection(n::zeros({2},n::DType::Int32));
#' const auto net=n::models::MaskedAutoencoder(p,n::zeros({2,28,28,1}),s);
#' const auto loss=n::models::MaeLoss(net.prediction,net.patches,net.mask);
#' // p.count() is 13649. The caller owns Adam and the minibatch loop.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid inputs or incompatible stored leaves.
#' @references - He, Chen, Xie, Li, Dollar and Girshick (2021/2022), Masked Autoencoders Are
#'   Scalable Vision Learners, https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.06377 . This MNIST model
#'   changes depth, widths, patches, sampler and sigmoid/raw-pixel loss; it is not
#'   an ImageNet or transfer-learning reproduction.
#' @seealso MaeEncode(), MaeDecode(), MaeLoss()
#' @md
#'
`MaskedAutoencoder` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_367`, NULL, list(...))
#' Average squared reconstruction error over explicitly hidden patch pixels only.
#'
#' Computes sum((prediction-patches)^2*mask\[...,None\])/(D*sum(mask)), for
#' \[B,P,D\] patch arrays and binary \[B,P\] hidden indicators. No stop-gradient is
#' applied to predictions or targets; mask must be detached. Values are not
#' range-checked: nonfinite arithmetic can propagate even through zero weights.
#' Shapes must be nonempty and at least one patch hidden. Strides/continuous
#' gradients are retained, with no mutation or shared state. Work/storage O(BPD).
#' @usage
#' MaeLoss(prediction, patches, mask)
#' @param prediction `Tensor`. Float32/64 predicted patch pixels \[B,P,D\].
#' @param patches `Tensor`. Matching clean target patch pixels.
#' @param mask `Tensor`. Matching detached binary hidden indicators \[B,P\], one meaning hidden.
#' @return `Tensor`: Differentiable scalar masked pixel MSE, normalized by hidden pixel count.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' const auto loss=n::models::MaeLoss(n::zeros({1,2,2}),n::ones({1,2,2}),
#'                                   n::tensor({0.,1.},{1,2}));
#' // loss is 1: only the second patch's two pixels are scored.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid shapes/dtypes/mask values or no hidden patches.
#' @references - He et al. (2021/2022), https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.06377 . This variant uses
#'   raw pixel targets without per-patch target normalization.
#' @seealso MaskedAutoencoder(), MaePatches()
#' @md
#'
`MaeLoss` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_368`, NULL, list(...))
#' Keep visible patch values and replace hidden patches with one explicit constant.
#'
#' For hidden\[b,p\]=1, every coordinate is fill; for zero, every original value
#' is copied bit-for-bit, including signed zero. Finite signed Float32/64
#' patches\[B,P,D\] and a detached matching-dtype binary mask\[B,P\] are required.
#' Every dimension is positive; all-visible and all-hidden masks are allowed.
#' All input values must be finite, including unselected hidden coordinates.
#' No pixel-range restriction, clipping, encoder, target or fitting is added.
#' Float32 narrows fill once; finite underflow is accepted, overflow rejected.
#' Strides are accepted. Visible input derivatives remain live; hidden input
#' derivatives are zero. Output owns lifetime; inputs unchanged, no shared
#' state or random draws. Work/storage O(B*P*D), plus O(B*P) mask conversion.
#' @usage
#' VisiblePatchValues(patches, hidden, fill = 0.0)
#' @param patches `Tensor`. Finite Float32/64 patch values\[B,P,D\], B,P,D positive.
#' @param hidden `Tensor`. Detached same-dtype binary mask\[B,P\], one meaning hidden.
#' @param fill `numeric`. Finite replacement representable without overflow, default0.
#' @return `Tensor`: Owning patch array of the same shape/dtype, with visible values preserved exactly.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' const auto visible=n::models::VisiblePatchValues(n::ones({1,2,3}),n::tensor({0.,1.},{1,2}));
#' // First patch is[1,1,1]; second patch is[0,0,0]. No hidden target is used.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid tensors/mask or nonfinite/unrepresentable fill.
#' @seealso CompositePatchValues(), MaePatches(), MaeImage()
#' @md
#'
`VisiblePatchValues` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_369`, NULL, list(...))
#' Fill hidden patches from predictions while copying observed patches exactly.
#'
#' Selects prediction at hidden\[b,p\]=1 and observed at zero. Matching finite
#' signed Float32/64\[B,P,D\] arrays and a detached same-dtype binary mask\[B,P\]
#' are required, with B,P,D positive. All-visible/all-hidden masks and strides
#' are accepted; every input value must be finite, including unselected ones.
#' Branch selection preserves selected value bits, including signed zero;
#' no averaging, blending, clamping or target values enter. Inputs unchanged,
#' owning same-shape/dtype output, no shared state, fitting or RNG. Continuous
#' first/second paths remain live in selected branches; unselected branch
#' derivatives are zero. Work/storage O(B*P*D), plus O(B*P) mask conversion.
#' @usage
#' CompositePatchValues(observed, prediction, hidden)
#' @param observed `Tensor`. Finite Float32/64 observed patch values\[B,P,D\].
#' @param prediction `Tensor`. Finite exactly matching-shape/dtype predicted patch values.
#' @param hidden `Tensor`. Detached same-dtype binary mask\[B,P\], one selecting prediction.
#' @return `Tensor`: Owning composite patches, with all visible observed coordinates bit-exact.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' const auto out=n::models::CompositePatchValues(n::zeros({1,2,3}),n::ones({1,2,3}),
#'     n::tensor({0.,1.},{1,2}));
#' // First patch is observed zeros; second patch is predicted ones.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid shapes/dtypes/values or a live/nonbinary mask.
#' @seealso VisiblePatchValues(), MaePatches(), MaeImage(), MaeLoss()
#' @md
#'
`CompositePatchValues` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_370`, NULL, list(...))
#' Sum stable Bernoulli cross-entropy over each row's pixels.
#'
#' Computes sum_j(softplus(logits_ij)-target_ij*logits_ij). Targets are finite
#' \[0,1\] values; binary values give Bernoulli negative log likelihood and soft
#' targets give cross-entropy. No pixel averaging, sigmoid clipping or Gaussian
#' KL is added. Finite Float32/64 matrices must share nonempty \[B,F\] shape/dtype.
#' Strides and gradients through both arguments are preserved; inputs are not
#' mutated. Output owns lifetime; work/storage O(B*F), no shared mutable state.
#' Finite large logits use softplus, avoiding log(sigmoid) underflow; numerical
#' overflow/cancellation in the computation dtype is not repaired.
#' @usage
#' BernoulliPixelNll(logits, target)
#' @param logits `Tensor`. Finite floating log-odds \[B,F\].
#' @param target `Tensor`. Finite same-shape/dtype target values in \[0,1\].
#' @return `Tensor`: Owning per-row summed cross-entropy \[B\], in the computation dtype.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' const auto values=n::models::BernoulliPixelNll(n::zeros({2,3}),n::zeros({2,3}));
#' // Each row is 3*log(2), approximately 2.07944, not a per-pixel mean.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid shapes, dtype or values.
#' @references - Makhzani, Shlens, Jaitly, Goodfellow and Frey (2015/2016), Adversarial
#'   Autoencoders, https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.05644 . This component supplies the
#'   migrated Bernoulli-decoder experiment's summed reconstruction term only.
#' @seealso AdversarialAutoencoder(), softplus()
#' @md
#'
`BernoulliPixelNll` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_371`, NULL, list(...))
#' Encode unit-range pixel rows with an independent tanh/linear deterministic network.
#'
#' Uses hidden/W\[features,hidden\], hidden/b\[hidden\], code/W\[hidden,latent\] and
#' code/b\[latent\], with ordinary Glorot-uniform weights and zero biases. There
#' is no posterior noise, KL term, fitting or normalization. Input checks precede
#' leaf creation; incompatible stored leaves can fail later. Strided matching
#' Float32/64 pixels preserve input and parameter gradients. Returned codes own
#' lifetime and can be signed/unbounded. Stored/computed nonfinite values are
#' not sanitized; shared stores need external synchronization. Work
#' O(B*hidden*(features+latent)), activation storage O(B*(hidden+latent)).
#' @usage
#' AaeEncode(encoder, image, features = 784, latent = 4, hidden = 16)
#' @param encoder `ParameterStore`. Mutable matching floating store, separate from other AAE groups.
#' @param image `Tensor`. Finite nonempty \[B,features\] values in \[0,1\].
#' @param features `integer`. Image width, default784.
#' @param latent `integer`. Code width, default4.
#' @param hidden `integer`. Positive tanh width, default16.
#' @return `Tensor`: Owning differentiable linear code \[B,latent\].
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' dm::nn::ParameterStore e(3501);
#' const auto code=dm::nn::models::AaeEncode(e,dm::nn::zeros({2,784}));
#' // code is [2,4]; encoder owns four leaves/12628 scalars.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid widths/input/dtype or incompatible leaves.
#' @references - Makhzani et al. (2015/2016), Adversarial Autoencoders,
#'   https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.05644 . This small deterministic encoder is an
#'   educational architecture, not the published benchmark configuration.
#' @seealso AaeDecode(), AdversarialAutoencoder()
#' @md
#'
`AaeEncode` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_372`, NULL, list(...))
#' Decode explicit codes into Bernoulli pixel logits through a tanh/linear network.
#'
#' Creates/reuses hidden/W\[latent,hidden\], hidden/b\[hidden\], output/W\[hidden,features\]
#' and output/b\[features\] with Glorot-uniform weights and zero biases. Only code
#' enters: no image, target, discriminator, copying, fitting or sigmoid is hidden.
#' Signed/zero finite codes are accepted. Strided matching Float32/64 inputs retain
#' code/parameter gradients and output ownership. Validation precedes creation;
#' incompatible leaves may fail later and nonfinite parameters/computed values
#' propagate. Shared stores need synchronization. Work
#' O(B*hidden*(latent+features)), activation storage O(B*(hidden+features)).
#' @usage
#' AaeDecode(decoder, code, features = 784, latent = 4, hidden = 16)
#' @param decoder `ParameterStore`. Mutable floating store, separate from the encoder.
#' @param code `Tensor`. Finite nonempty \[B,latent\] code.
#' @param features `integer`. Output width, default784.
#' @param latent `integer`. Code width, default4.
#' @param hidden `integer`. Positive tanh width, default16.
#' @return `Tensor`: Owning differentiable logits \[B,features\]; call sigmoid() for probabilities.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore g(3511);
#' const auto pixels=n::sigmoid(n::models::AaeDecode(g,n::zeros({2,4})));
#' // pixels are [2,784]; decoder owns four leaves/13408 scalars.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid widths/code/dtype or incompatible leaves.
#' @references - Makhzani et al. (2015/2016), https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.05644 . Uses the
#'   migrated source's small Bernoulli decoder; outputs are probabilities, not sampled pixels.
#' @seealso AaeEncode(), BernoulliPixelNll(), AdversarialAutoencoder()
#' @md
#'
`AaeDecode` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_373`, NULL, list(...))
#' Evaluate latent prior-versus-encoded logits through an independent tanh discriminator.
#'
#' Uses hidden/W\[latent,hidden\], hidden/b\[hidden\], logit/W\[hidden,1\] and logit/b\[1\]
#' with ordinary Glorot-uniform weights and zero biases (97 default scalars).
#' No labels, sampling, detachment, loss, clipping or optimizer is hidden. Matching
#' finite Float32/64 rows, signed/zero codes and strides are accepted. Returns an
#' owning differentiable value retaining code and discriminator gradients. Input
#' validation precedes creation; incompatible leaves may fail later. Nonfinite
#' stored/computed values propagate; shared stores need synchronization. Work
#' O(B*hidden*latent), activation storage O(B*hidden).
#' @usage
#' AaeDiscriminate(discriminator, code, latent = 4, hidden = 16)
#' @param discriminator `ParameterStore`. Mutable floating store, separate from encoder and decoder.
#' @param code `Tensor`. Finite nonempty \[B,latent\] prior or encoded rows.
#' @param latent `integer`. Positive code width, default4.
#' @param hidden `integer`. Positive tanh width, default16.
#' @return `Tensor`: Owning differentiable log-odds \[B,1\], with positive meaning prior/real.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' dm::nn::ParameterStore d(3527);
#' const auto logits=dm::nn::models::AaeDiscriminate(d,dm::nn::zeros({2,4}));
#' // logits are [2,1]; no probabilities or labels are supplied to the network.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid widths/code/dtype or incompatible leaves.
#' @references - Makhzani et al. (2015/2016), https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.05644 . Labels in
#'   the caller's objective assign prior=real and encoded=fake.
#' @seealso AdversarialAutoencoder(), softplus()
#' @md
#'
`AaeDiscriminate` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_374`, NULL, list(...))
#' Evaluate a deterministic AAE and all three explicit reconstruction/adversarial objectives.
#'
#' Uses independent AaeEncode(), AaeDecode() and AaeDiscriminate() groups. Real
#' discriminator examples come from explicit prior rows; encoded examples are
#' fake. Reconstruction sums Bernoulli NLL over features then averages images.
#' Discriminator loss averages prior and encoded softplus terms separately,
#' allowing P!=B; encoder adversarial loss uses non-saturating real labels.
#' The 784–16–4 /4–16–784 /4–16–1 default has12 leaves/26133 scalars.
#' There is no Gaussian posterior, per-example KL, ELBO, RNG or hidden training.
#' Prior changes affect discriminator outputs/objectives only, not code or pixels.
#'
#' All fields own lifetime and retain all continuous paths, including target
#' derivatives when image requires gradients and encoder gradients of discriminator
#' loss. No group is detached implicitly: callers choose requested gradients and
#' updates. Compute encoder/decoder reconstruction gradients from one snapshot
#' before either update, then recompute for discriminator and encoder-adversarial
#' phases, with four independent optimizer states if following the source protocol.
#' Validation of widths, image/prior, dtype and distinct store identities precedes
#' leaf creation. Incompatible leaves or nonfinite computed logits may fail later.
#' Inputs are not mutated; shared stores require synchronization. Work/storage are
#' those of the three small networks; separate prior batch work is O(P*hidden*latent).
#' @usage
#' AdversarialAutoencoder(encoder, decoder, discriminator, image, prior, features = 784, latent = 4, hidden = 16)
#' @param encoder `ParameterStore`. Mutable floating store, distinct from decoder/discriminator.
#' @param decoder `ParameterStore`. Mutable same-dtype floating store, distinct from other groups.
#' @param discriminator `ParameterStore`. Mutable same-dtype floating store, distinct from other groups.
#' @param image `Tensor`. Finite nonempty unit-range \[B,features\] Float32/64 pixel rows.
#' @param prior `Tensor`. Finite same-dtype \[P,latent\] prior rows, P>0, supplied by caller.
#' @param features `integer`. Positive image width, default784.
#' @param latent `integer`. Positive code width, default4.
#' @param hidden `integer`. Positive tanh width in each network, default16.
#' @return `AaeOutput`: Owning nine-field forward value, with three distinct scalar losses.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore e(3501),g(3511),d(3527);
#' const auto x=n::full({2,784},.2),prior=n::zeros({2,4});
#' const auto out=n::models::AdversarialAutoencoder(e,g,d,x,prior);
#' auto variables=e.parameters();const auto dec=g.parameters();
#' variables.insert(variables.end(),dec.begin(),dec.end());
#' const auto reconstruction_gradients=n::grad(out.reconstruction_loss,variables);
#' // Compute BOTH groups before writing either. Optimizers/phase loops belong to the caller.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid input/options, aliased/mismatched stores,
#'   incompatible leaves or nonfinite logits/encoded values encountered later.
#' @references - Makhzani, Shlens, Jaitly, Goodfellow and Frey (2015/2016), Adversarial
#'   Autoencoders, https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.05644 . This deterministic tanh,
#'   Bernoulli-decoder teaching model matches an aggregate prior adversarially;
#'   finite training does not certify distributional matching or useful generation.
#' @seealso AaeOutput, AaeEncode(), AaeDecode(), AaeDiscriminate(), BernoulliPixelNll()
#' @md
#'
`AdversarialAutoencoder` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_375`, NULL, list(...))
#' Extract ReLU features with explicit frozen weights and a live input path.
#'
#' Computes relu(image*weight+bias). The supplied weight/bias are detached, so
#' only image derivatives remain live; this is frozen transfer, not fine-tuning.
#' Finite signed matching Float32/64 matrices accept strides. No targets,
#' standardization, parameter creation, fitting or RNG is involved. Result owns
#' lifetime, inputs unchanged; callers synchronize concurrent input mutation.
#' ReLU uses the core's zero subgradient at zero. Computed overflow is not
#' repaired. Work O(B*F*L), activation storage O(B*L).
#' @usage
#' FrozenEncoderFeatures(image, weight, bias)
#' @param image `Tensor`. Finite matching pixel/feature rows\[B,F\], B,F positive.
#' @param weight `Tensor`. Finite frozen matrix\[F,L\], L positive.
#' @param bias `Tensor`. Finite matching frozen vector\[L\].
#' @return `Tensor`: Owning code\[B,L\], with input gradients and stopped weight/bias paths.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' const auto code=n::models::FrozenEncoderFeatures(n::ones({2,3}),n::ones({3,2}),n::zeros({2}));
#' // code[2,2] contains threes; supplied encoder leaves are not trained.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid inputs or dimension products.
#' @references - Vincent et al. (2010), Stacked Denoising Autoencoders,
#'   https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v11/vincent10a.html . This migrated teaching
#'   variant uses one frozen ReLU encoder rather than stacked supervised fine-tuning.
#' @seealso FitTransferScaling(), FrozenEncoderClassifier
#' @md
