`Vq2TopDecoder` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1019`, NULL, list(...))
#' Decode fine code vectors and the coarse conditioning feature to Bernoulli logits.
#'
#' Concatenates bottom\[B*h*w,D\] reshaped to\[B,h,w,D\] with top_feature\[B,h,w,D\],
#' then same Conv3 2D->C/ReLU, repeat2, same Conv3 C->1/linear. h,w must be
#' positive even dimensions. Uses decoder/W,b (He/zero) and output/W,b
#' (ordinary Glorot/zero); default four leaves contain657 scalars. Both inputs
#' are code-derived; no encoder/image/target enters. No sigmoid, fitting, random
#' bank, detach or mode change is hidden. Finite matching Float32/64 inputs
#' accept strides and retain all continuous code/feature/leaf paths. Input and
#' product checks precede leaves; incompatible leaves can fail later. Owning
#' output; inputs unchanged. Work O(B*h*w*9*C*(2D+4)); synchronize shared stores.
#' @usage
#' Vq2Decoder(parameters, bottom, top_feature, channels = 8)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable matching floating store containing/creating decoder/output leaves.
#' @param bottom `Tensor`. Finite flattened fine vectors\[B*h*w,D\].
#' @param top_feature `Tensor`. Finite matching coarse-derived features\[B,h,w,D\], h,w even.
#' @param channels `integer`. Positive decoder hidden channels, default8.
#' @return `Tensor`: Owning differentiable logits\[B,4*h*w\], without probability clipping.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(2801);
#' const auto feature=n::models::Vq2TopDecoder(p,n::zeros({49,4}));
#' const auto probability=n::sigmoid(n::models::Vq2Decoder(p,n::zeros({196,4}),feature));
#' // Code-only probability[1,784]; six shared decoder leaves in total.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid shape/dtype/values/products or incompatible leaves.
#' @references - Razavi et al. (2019), https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.00446 . Uses a compact
#'   two-scale feed-forward decoder, not the paper's full architecture.
#' @seealso Vq2TopDecoder(), VqLookup(), VectorQuantizedAutoencoder2()
#' @md
#'
`Vq2Decoder` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1020`, NULL, list(...))
#' Evaluate the source-preserving two-scale VQ-VAE with an optional discrete top override.
#'
#' Same Conv3 1->D/ReLU, avg2 gives fine encoder h; same Conv3 D->D/ReLU,
#' avg2 gives coarse vectors. Top dictionary lookup feeds Vq2TopDecoder().
#' A linear Conv1 projection of concat(h,top_feature) gives fine vectors and
#' an independent fine dictionary. Vq2Decoder() sees only fine quantized
#' vectors and coarse code-derived features. There is no continuous image bypass
#' to decoding; fine ENCODING is deliberately conditioned on top codes.
#' Creation order is encoder1,encoder2,top_codebook,top_decoder,bottom_projection,
#' bottom_codebook,decoder,output (each layer W then b). Dictionaries are U(-.5,.5),
#' ReLU kernels He-uniform, linear kernels ordinary Glorot, biases zero. Default
#' K16/D4/C8 yields14 leaves/1157 scalars. Loss=mean(pixel-summed NLL)+
#' ((top_dictionary+bottom_dictionary)+beta*(top_commitment+bottom_commitment));
#' each dictionary/commitment penalty averages ALL vectors/coordinates at its own
#' resolution. Reconstruction skips both books and copies surrogate gradients
#' to encoders; fine losses can reach the coarse encoder through top_feature.
#' No EMA, resets, Gaussian KL, fitting loop, mode change or random bank is hidden.
#' Parameter creation uses only the caller's initializer stream. Optional valid
#' Int32 top IDs replace assignments without disconnecting their encoder surrogate.
#' Finite matching Float32/64 unit-range grayscale NHWC accepts strides. Width,
#' override and input checks precede leaves; incompatible leaves or computed
#' overflow can fail later. Inputs unchanged, owning outputs. Dominant conv work
#' O(BHW*9*(D^2+D*C+C)); distance work O(BHW*K*D), with O(BHW*K) assignment storage.
#' Synchronize callers mutating a shared store. Surrogate derivatives are not
#' derivatives of hard nearest assignment; higher paths follow the surrogate.
#' @usage
#' VectorQuantizedAutoencoder2(parameters, image, top_indices = Tensor$new(), entries = 16, dimensions = 4, channels = 8, commitment_beta = 0.25)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable matching floating store creating/reusing all fourteen leaves.
#' @param image `Tensor`. Finite unit-range nonempty Float32/64\[B,H,W,1\], H,W divisible by four.
#' @param top_indices `Tensor`. Optional valid Int32 tensor with B*H*W/16 elements, any shape; undefined
#'   selects nearest.
#' @param entries `integer`. Positive vocabulary size for each book, default16.
#' @param dimensions `integer`. Positive embedding width, default4.
#' @param channels `integer`. Positive final decoder hidden width, default8.
#' @param commitment_beta `numeric`. Finite nonnegative representable multiplier, default0.25.
#' @return `VectorQuantizedAutoencoder2Output`: Owning eleven-field model record, including two six-field quantizers.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(2801);
#' const auto out=n::models::VectorQuantizedAutoencoder2(p,n::full({2,28,28,1},.3));
#' const auto gradients=n::grad(out.loss,p.parameters());
#' // The caller applies all fourteen derivatives; no optimizer step is hidden.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid input/override/options/products or incompatible
#'   leaves.
#' @references - Razavi, van den Oord and Vinyals (2019), Generating Diverse High-Fidelity
#'   Images with VQ-VAE-2, https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.00446 . This compact model
#'   uses gradient dictionary updates and Bernoulli pixel NLL, not the paper's
#'   EMA dictionary updates, squared pixel loss or full-resolution architecture.
#' @seealso VectorQuantize(), Vq2TopDecoder(), Vq2Decoder(), Vq2ConditionalPrior
#' @md
#'
`VectorQuantizedAutoencoder2` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1021`, NULL, list(...))
#' Evaluate the equally averaged IMQ kernels with scales1,4,16 for every sample pair.
#'
#' Returns mean_c c/(c+sum_d(x_i\[d\]-y_j\[d\])^2), c in\{1,4,16\}. Finite nonempty
#' same-width/dtype Float32/64 matrices may have unequal row counts and strides.
#' All x/y derivatives, including second derivatives, remain live. Self-pair
#' diagonals are exactly one for finite computed differences. No bandwidth fit,
#' normalization by dimension, RNG or clipping is hidden. Inputs unchanged;
#' result owns lifetime, no shared state. Work/storage O(N*M*D), materializing
#' pairwise differences; int64 product overflow is rejected before allocation.
#' Squared-distance overflow is not rescaled and can invalidate gradients.
#' @usage
#' ImqKernel(x, y)
#' @param x `Tensor`. Finite first sample\[N,D\], N,D positive.
#' @param y `Tensor`. Finite matching second sample\[M,D\], M positive.
#' @return `Tensor`: Owning differentiable kernel matrix\[N,M\].
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' const auto k=n::models::ImqKernel(n::zeros({2,1}),n::ones({3,1}));
#' // Every entry is(1/2+4/5+16/17)/3, approximately0.7470588.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid shape/dtype/values or dimension products.
#' @references - Zhao, Song and Ermon (2017/2018), InfoVAE: Information Maximizing Variational
#'   Autoencoders, https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.02262 . Uses a small fixed three-scale
#'   IMQ adaptation from the migrated teaching experiment, not a fitted bandwidth.
#' @seealso ImqMmd(), MmdOutput
#' @md
#'
`ImqKernel` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1022`, NULL, list(...))
#' Compute an unclipped unbiased two-sample MMD squared using fixed averaged IMQ kernels.
#'
#' Uses independent samples x\[N,D\], y\[M,D\], with N,M>=2 and arbitrary unequal
#' counts. Excludes only within-sample diagonals; cross pairs are all included.
#' Self-comparison of the same nonconstant array can therefore be negative:
#' unbiasedness refers to independent sample sets, not a nonnegative finite
#' V-statistic. No sqrt, clipping or test calibration is applied. Coefficients
#' and reduction parenthesization preserve the original Float32 calculation.
#'
#' Finite matching Float32/64 widths/dtypes, signed or zero values and strides
#' are accepted. First/second derivatives through both samples stay live. Inputs
#' unchanged; every result owns lifetime. Work/storage O((N^2+M^2+N*M)*D);
#' products are checked before kernels, but arithmetic overflow is not repaired.
#' @usage
#' ImqMmd(x, y)
#' @param x `Tensor`. Finite first sample\[N,D\], N>=2,D>0.
#' @param y `Tensor`. Finite matching second sample\[M,D\], M>=2.
#' @return `MmdOutput`: Owning seven-field kernel/within/cross/signed-value result.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' const auto x=n::tensor({0.,1.},{2,1});
#' const auto out=n::models::ImqMmd(x,x);
#' // Negative value: excluding within diagonals differs from the full cross mean.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid samples or pairwise dimension products.
#' @references - Gretton et al. (2012), A Kernel Two-Sample Test, JMLR13,723-773,
#'   https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v13/gretton12a.html . Uses the unbiased quadratic estimator.
#' - Zhao, Song and Ermon (2017/2018), https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.02262 .
#' @seealso MmdOutput, ImqKernel(), InfoVariationalAutoencoder()
#' @md
#'
`ImqMmd` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1023`, NULL, list(...))
#' Sum squared unit-range pixel differences independently for each image.
#'
#' Returns sum_j(image_ij-probability_ij)^2, with no pixel averaging or clipping.
#' Both arguments are finite matching nonempty Float32/64\[B,F\] matrices in\[0,1\].
#' This is a squared reconstruction cost, not a Gaussian/Bernoulli log density.
#' Strides, first/second derivatives through both inputs and owning lifetime
#' are preserved. Inputs unchanged; no state, RNG or fitting. Work/storage O(B*F).
#' @usage
#' WaeSquaredPixelCost(image, probability)
#' @param image `Tensor`. Finite unit-range targets\[B,F\], B,F positive.
#' @param probability `Tensor`. Finite same-shape/dtype decoder values in\[0,1\].
#' @return `Tensor`: Owning per-image squared pixel sums\[B\], in the input dtype.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' const auto cost=n::models::WaeSquaredPixelCost(n::zeros({2,4}),n::full({2,4},.5));
#' // cost is[1,1]: four squared errors of .25 per image.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid shapes, dtype or values.
#' @references - Tolstikhin, Bousquet, Gelly and Schoelkopf (2018), Wasserstein Auto-Encoders,
#'   https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.01558 . Uses the pixel-summed squared cost.
#' @seealso WaeObjective(), WassersteinAutoencoder()
#' @md
#'
`WaeSquaredPixelCost` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1024`, NULL, list(...))
#' Average per-image squared cost and add explicitly weighted signed MMD squared.
#'
#' Computes mean(squared_cost)+weight*mmd. Cost is a finite nonnegative nonempty
#' Float32/64\[B\] vector; mmd is one finite matching-dtype value and may be negative.
#' No square root or nonnegative clipping is applied. Zero weight retains a zero
#' derivative to mmd. Float32 narrows weight before multiplication; positive
#' underflow/overflow is rejected. This penalized finite-sample objective is not
#' an exact Wasserstein distance, image likelihood or negative ELBO. Inputs
#' unchanged, owning scalar and live first/second derivatives. Work O(B); no state.
#' @usage
#' WaeObjective(squared_cost, mmd, weight = 10.0)
#' @param squared_cost `Tensor`. Finite nonnegative per-image pixel sums\[B\], B>0.
#' @param mmd `Tensor`. Finite signed scalar/one-element aggregate discrepancy, matching dtype.
#' @param weight `numeric`. Finite nonnegative coefficient, default10.
#' @return `Tensor`: Owning scalar mean reconstruction plus weighted signed MMD.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' const auto loss=n::models::WaeObjective(n::tensor({2.,4.}),n::scalar(-.1));
#' // mean([2,4])+10*(-.1) = 2; negative discrepancy is retained.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid tensors or unrepresentable controls.
#' @references - Tolstikhin et al. (2018), Wasserstein Auto-Encoders,
#'   https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.01558 . Uses the WAE-MMD penalized objective.
#' @seealso WaeSquaredPixelCost(), ImqMmd(), WassersteinAutoencoder()
#' @md
#'
`WaeObjective` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1025`, NULL, list(...))
#' Decode explicit deterministic WAE codes through a tanh/linear network.
#'
#' Creates/reuses decoder/W\[L,H\],decoder/b\[H\],output/W\[H,F\],output/b\[F\], in that
#' order, with ordinary Glorot-uniform weights and zero biases. Four default
#' leaves contain13,408 scalars and are shared with WassersteinAutoencoder().
#' Only supplied codes enter; no image, encoder, prior draws, MMD, sigmoid or fit
#' is hidden. Apply sigmoid() for unit-range decoder values. Strided finite
#' signed/zero Float32/64 codes retain input/parameter derivatives and owning
#' lifetime. Validation precedes leaves; incompatible existing leaves can fail
#' later. Computed overflow is not repaired. Inputs unchanged; synchronize shared
#' stores. Work O(B*H*(L+F)), activation storage O(B*(H+F)).
#' @usage
#' WaeDecode(parameters, code, features = 784, latent = 4, hidden = 16)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable matching floating store, containing/creating decoder leaves.
#' @param code `Tensor`. Finite nonempty code matrix\[B,latent\].
#' @param features `integer`. Positive output width, default784.
#' @param latent `integer`. Positive code width, default4.
#' @param hidden `integer`. Positive tanh width, default16.
#' @return `Tensor`: Owning differentiable linear logits\[B,features\].
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(3701);
#' const auto images=n::sigmoid(n::models::WaeDecode(p,n::zeros({20,4})));
#' // Twenty code-only decoder values[20,784]; no pixel sampling is performed.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid tensors/widths/products or incompatible leaves.
#' @references - Tolstikhin et al. (2018), https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.01558 . This is the
#'   migrated small tanh teaching architecture, not the paper's benchmark network.
#' @seealso WassersteinAutoencoder(), WaeOutput
#' @md
#'
`WaeDecode` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1026`, NULL, list(...))
#' Evaluate a deterministic tanh WAE with explicit independent-prior MMD regularization.
#'
#' Encodes image through encoder tanh then linear code, decodes through WaeDecode
#' and sigmoid, and returns pixel-summed squared costs plus signed ImqMmd(code,prior).
#' Defaults F784/L4/H16 create eight leaves/26,036 scalars in source order:
#' encoder/W,b; code/W,b; decoder/W,b; output/W,b. Ordinary Glorot-uniform weights
#' and zero biases; source seed3701. No per-image Gaussian posterior, KL,
#' Bernoulli training loss, discriminator, sampling, optimizer or fitting loop.
#'
#' Image and prior counts B,P>=2 can differ; even weight0 computes full MMD and
#' requires these counts. Prior values do not affect codes or reconstructions.
#' Independent standard-normal sampling and fitting-only provenance are caller
#' responsibilities; no array-level check establishes statistical independence.
#' IMQ averages scales1,4,16 without dimension scaling. Within estimates exclude
#' diagonals, cross includes all pairs; a negative finite-sample estimate is
#' retained. This teaching variant is not an exact transport-distance solver.
#'
#' Finite matching Float32/64 strided inputs preserve image, prior and parameter
#' first/second derivatives and all owning result lifetimes. Widths, controls,
#' matrices and pairwise/architecture products are checked before leaves.
#' Incompatible existing leaves or nonfinite computed values can fail later;
#' no rollback or overflow repair. Inputs unchanged; synchronize shared stores.
#' Work includes O(B*H*(F+L)+(B^2+P^2+B*P)*L), materializing pairwise differences.
#' @usage
#' WassersteinAutoencoder(parameters, image, prior, weight = 10.0, features = 784, latent = 4, hidden = 16)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable matching floating store; original seed3701.
#' @param image `Tensor`. Finite unit-range pixels\[B,features\], B>=2.
#' @param prior `Tensor`. Explicit finite independent standard-normal samples\[P,latent\], P>=2.
#' @param weight `numeric`. Finite nonnegative signed-MMD coefficient, default10.
#' @param features `integer`. Positive pixel width, default784.
#' @param latent `integer`. Positive code width, default4.
#' @param hidden `integer`. Positive tanh width, default16.
#' @return `WaeOutput`: Owning six-tensor plus nested MMD output, without fitting or random prior draws.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(3701);
#' const auto out=n::models::WassersteinAutoencoder(p,n::full({2,784},.2),n::zeros({3,4}));
#' const auto derivatives=n::grad(out.loss,p.parameters());
#' // Caller applies its own Adam step to all eight leaves.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid inputs/options/products or incompatible leaves.
#' @references - Tolstikhin, Bousquet, Gelly and Schoelkopf (2018), Wasserstein Auto-Encoders,
#'   ICLR2018, https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.01558 . Uses a compact deterministic
#'   WAE-MMD teaching model, fixed three-scale averaged IMQ kernels and weight10.
#' @seealso WaeOutput, WaeDecode(), WaeSquaredPixelCost(), WaeObjective(), ImqMmd()
#' @md
#'
`WassersteinAutoencoder` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1027`, NULL, list(...))
#' Sum squared discriminator-feature residuals against a detached real target.
#'
#' Returns .5*sum((real.detach()-reconstruction)^2,axis=1). Both arguments are
#' finite matching nonempty Float32/64 \[B,D\] matrices; values need not be pixels
#' or bounded. Feature coordinates are summed, not averaged. The unit-variance
#' Gaussian normalizing constant D*log(2*pi)/2 is omitted. This is a learned
#' feature-space residual, not an image-space likelihood or pixel ELBO.
#' Real targets are detached at every derivative order; reconstructed features
#' retain first/higher gradients and strides. Inputs are unchanged; output owns
#' lifetime. O(B*D) work/storage, without shared mutable state. Computation-dtype
#' overflow is not repaired.
#' @usage
#' VaeGanFeatureNll(real, reconstruction)
#' @param real `Tensor`. Finite floating real-image feature targets \[B,D\].
#' @param reconstruction `Tensor`. Finite matching reconstructed-image features \[B,D\].
#' @return `Tensor`: Owning differentiable \[B\] half-summed squared feature distances.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' const auto distance=n::models::VaeGanFeatureNll(n::ones({2,3}),n::zeros({2,3}));
#' // Each row is 1.5, without the Gaussian normalizing constant.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid shape, dtype or nonfinite values.
#' @references - Larsen, Sønderby, Larochelle and Winther (2016), Autoencoding beyond pixels
#'   using a learned similarity metric, https://proceedings.mlr.press/v48/larsen16.html ,
#'   equations 6-7. Target detachment follows the migrated teaching implementation.
#' @seealso VaeGan(), VaeGanDiscriminator()
#' @md
#'
`VaeGanFeatureNll` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1028`, NULL, list(...))
#' Decode explicit Gaussian codes with the source tanh/sigmoid generator.
