#' Reuses AaeDecode()'s hidden/W\[latent,hidden\], hidden/b\[hidden\],
#' output/W\[hidden,features\], output/b\[features\], then applies sigmoid. Ordinary
#' Glorot-uniform weights and zero biases give four leaves/13408 default scalars.
#' Only codes enter; there is no image, posterior, RNG, detach, fit or optimizer.
#' Finite signed/zero nonempty matching Float32/64 rows and strides retain code
#' and generator gradients. Checks precede creation; incompatible leaves or
#' nonfinite computed states can fail later. Outputs own lifetime, with no
#' input mutation. Work O(P*hidden*(latent+features)); activation storage
#' O(P*(hidden+features)). Synchronize callers sharing a mutable store.
#' @usage
#' VaeGanDecode(generator, code, features = 784, latent = 4, hidden = 16)
#' @param generator `ParameterStore`. Mutable floating generator store.
#' @param code `Tensor`. Finite nonempty \[P,latent\] codes matching the store dtype.
#' @param features `integer`. Positive output pixel width, default784.
#' @param latent `integer`. Positive code width, default4.
#' @param hidden `integer`. Positive tanh width, default16.
#' @return `Tensor`: Owning differentiable sigmoid probabilities \[P,features\], not pixel draws.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' dm::nn::ParameterStore g(3307);
#' const auto pixels=dm::nn::models::VaeGanDecode(g,dm::nn::zeros({2,4}));
#' // pixels is [2,784]; decoding requires no encoder or image.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid input/widths/products or incompatible leaves.
#' @references - Larsen et al. (2016), https://proceedings.mlr.press/v48/larsen16.html .
#'   This compact dense teaching generator differs from the paper's convolutional model.
#' @seealso VaeGan(), AaeDecode()
#' @md
#'
`VaeGanDecode` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1029`, NULL, list(...))
#' Compute source tanh discriminator features and linear real/fake logits.
#'
#' Creates/reuses feature/W\[features,feature_width\], feature/b\[feature_width\],
#' logit/W\[feature_width,1\] and logit/b\[1\] in that order, using Glorot-uniform
#' weights and zero biases (four leaves/6289 default scalars). Finite nonempty
#' matching Float32/64 matrices are accepted, including signed inputs and
#' strides; no pixel clipping, labels, sigmoid, detach or fit is hidden. Input
#' checks precede creation; incompatible leaves can fail later. Nonfinite computed
#' states propagate. Both owned outputs retain input/parameter gradients;
#' inputs are unchanged. Work O(P*features*feature_width), activation storage
#' O(P*feature_width). Synchronize shared stores.
#' @usage
#' VaeGanDiscriminator(discriminator, image, features = 784, feature_width = 8)
#' @param discriminator `ParameterStore`. Mutable floating discriminator store.
#' @param image `Tensor`. Finite nonempty matching-dtype rows \[P,features\].
#' @param features `integer`. Positive input width, default784.
#' @param feature_width `integer`. Positive tanh feature width, default8.
#' @return `VaeGanDiscriminatorOutput`: Owning feature and logit fields, without a loss or probabilities.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore d(3313,n::DType::Float64);
#' const auto out=n::models::VaeGanDiscriminator(d,n::full({3,2},-.2,n::DType::Float64),2,4);
#' // out.feature is [3,4]; signed inputs are allowed in this standalone component.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid inputs/widths/products or incompatible leaves.
#' @references - Larsen et al. (2016), https://proceedings.mlr.press/v48/larsen16.html .
#'   Features implement the compact teaching experiment's learned reconstruction metric.
#' @seealso VaeGanFeatureNll(), VaeGan()
#' @md
#'
`VaeGanDiscriminator` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1030`, NULL, list(...))
#' Evaluate a Gaussian VAE-GAN with explicit posterior noise and independent prior codes.
#'
#' Encoder: features->hidden tanh->mu/log_variance linear heads. Generator:
#' VaeGanDecode() is shared by posterior and prior codes. Discriminator:
#' VaeGanDiscriminator() is shared by real, reconstructed and prior pixels.
#' Creation order/names match the source: E hidden,mu,log_variance; G hidden,output;
#' D feature,logit (each W then b). Defaults give E12696+G13408+D6289=32393
#' scalars in 6+4+4 leaves. No images bypass the sampled latent into the generator.
#'
#' Encoder objective is mean(feature_nll+KL). Generator objective is
#' mean(feature_nll)+.5*(mean(softplus(-reconstruction_logit))+
#' mean(softplus(-prior_logit))). Discriminator objective is
#' mean(softplus(-real_logit))+.5*(mean(softplus(reconstruction_logit))+
#' mean(softplus(prior_logit))). Each branch averages its own rows, so P may
#' differ from B. These are separate objectives, not a single pixel ELBO.
#' The source uses unit feature weight and balanced, non-saturating fake branches.
#' Pixel NLL is only diagnostic: sigmoid probabilities are clipped to the
#' source's Float32-represented 1e-6 and 1-1e-6 endpoints, even in Float64.
#'
#' No fit, RNG, update, NoGradGuard or implicit parameter freezing is hidden.
#' Select only D leaves for discriminator_loss, E leaves for encoder_loss and
#' G leaves for generator_loss; recompute the forward after each written group.
#' Other mathematical paths remain live, except the real-feature target detach.
#' Finite unit-range images, finite noise/prior and all three distinct stores
#' must share Float32/64 dtype. Strides and continuous higher derivatives are
#' retained. Input/geometry checks precede leaf creation; incompatible leaves
#' or nonfinite intermediate states may fail later. Numerical overflow is not
#' repaired. Inputs are unchanged; returned fields own lifetime. Work
#' O((B+P)*features*(hidden+feature_width)+(B+P)*latent*hidden), activation
#' storage O((B+P)*(features+hidden+latent+feature_width)). Shared stores need
#' external synchronization.
#' @usage
#' VaeGan(encoder, generator, discriminator, image, epsilon, prior, features = 784, latent = 4, hidden = 16, feature_width = 8)
#' @param encoder `ParameterStore`. Mutable independent floating encoder store.
#' @param generator `ParameterStore`. Mutable independent generator store, matching encoder dtype.
#' @param discriminator `ParameterStore`. Mutable independent discriminator store, matching dtype.
#' @param image `Tensor`. Finite nonempty unit-range \[B,features\] pixel rows.
#' @param epsilon `Tensor`. Explicit finite standard-normal noise \[B,latent\], matching dtype.
#' @param prior `Tensor`. Explicit finite independent prior codes \[P,latent\], matching dtype.
#' @param features `integer`. Positive pixel width, default784.
#' @param latent `integer`. Positive Gaussian width, default4.
#' @param hidden `integer`. Positive encoder/generator tanh width, default16.
#' @param feature_width `integer`. Positive discriminator tanh width, default8.
#' @return `VaeGanOutput`: Owning nineteen-field VaeGanOutput, with three separate scalar objectives.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore e(3301),g(3307),d(3313);
#' const auto out=n::models::VaeGan(e,g,d,n::zeros({2,784}),n::zeros({2,4}),n::zeros({2,4}));
#' const auto de=n::grad(out.encoder_loss,e.parameters());
#' // Apply de only to E with a caller-owned optimizer; recompute before the next group.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid inputs/options/aliased stores or incompatible leaves.
#' @references - Larsen, Sønderby, Larochelle and Winther (2016), Autoencoding beyond pixels
#'   using a learned similarity metric, https://proceedings.mlr.press/v48/larsen16.html .
#'   This dense, balanced-fake, non-saturating teaching variant does not reproduce
#'   the paper's convolutional architecture or RMSProp benchmark training.
#' @seealso VaeGanDecode(), VaeGanDiscriminator(), VaeGanFeatureNll(), GaussianReparameterize()
#' @md
#'
`VaeGan` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1031`, NULL, list(...))
#' Diagnose heldout factor recovery from Gaussian posterior means with fitting-only probes.
#'
#' Fits population mean/std (floor1e-8), centered targets, all-axis ridge and a
#' single-axis ridge probe. The best axis maximizes absolute FIT Pearson correlation
#' (ties choose lowest index); test correlation never affects fitting. Ridge solves
#' (X'X+ridge I)C=X'Y in Float64 without penalizing the restored target mean.
#' Targets are numeric factor indices/continuous values, not class predictions.
#' Heldout R2 uses the heldout target mean in its denominator; negative values
#' remain visible. Constant fitting axes correlate zero; each test target must vary.
#' Mean KL per unit uses fitting mu/log variance only. This is neither a MIG/DCI
#' nor the beta-VAE paper's classifier metric and does not certify disentanglement.
#'
#' Returns13 named arrays: latent_mean/std, mu_variance, mean_kl_per_unit \[L\];
#' factor_mean \[K\]; fit/test_correlation and ridge_coefficients \[L,K\];
#' best_axis_by_fit \[K\] Int32; ridge/single_prediction \[Nt,K\]; ridge/single_r2 \[K\].
#' All other arrays are Float64, detached owning diagnostics. Inputs are unchanged;
#' no shared state. Work includes O(Nf*L^2+L^3+Nt*L*K); normal equations may be
#' ill-conditioned despite positive ridge. Nonfinite intermediate/output values
#' raise rather than silently returning unusable diagnostics.
#' @usage
#' LatentFactorDiagnostics(fit_mu_t, test_mu_t, fit_factors_t, test_factors_t, fit_log_variance_t, ridge = 0.001)
#' @param fit_mu_t `Tensor`. Finite nonempty Float32/64 fitting posterior means \[Nf,L\].
#' @param test_mu_t `Tensor`. Finite matching-dtype heldout means \[Nt,L\].
#' @param fit_factors_t `Tensor`. Finite numeric fitting targets \[Nf,K\], integer or floating.
#' @param test_factors_t `Tensor`. Finite numeric heldout targets \[Nt,K\], same target dtype.
#' @param fit_log_variance_t `Tensor`. Finite matching-shape/dtype fitting log variances \[Nf,L\].
#' @param ridge `numeric`. Finite positive diagonal penalty, default0.001.
#' @return `vector<list(character, Tensor)>`: Owning NamedTensors with13 detached arrays in the documented order.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' const auto fit=n::tensor({0.,1.,2.},{3,1});
#' const auto test=n::tensor({3.,4.},{2,1});
#' const auto diagnostic=n::models::LatentFactorDiagnostics(fit,test,fit,test,n::zeros({3,1}));
#' // diagnostic[8] selects axis0 using fitting labels only; all13 arrays own lifetime.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid inputs, constant test targets, nonfinite
#'   arithmetic or an invalid/underflowed ridge; solve() may reject singular systems.
#' @references - Hoerl and Kennard (1970), Ridge Regression: Biased Estimation for Nonorthogonal
#'   Problems, https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1970.10488634 .
#' - Kingma and Welling (2013/2014), https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.6114 , Gaussian KL.
#' @seealso BetaVariationalAutoencoder(), solve(), NamedTensors
#' @md
#'
`LatentFactorDiagnostics` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1032`, NULL, list(...))
#' Decode explicit Gaussian codes with a required supplied class and no image input.
#'
#' Concatenates code then one_hot(labels,classes) and applies a hidden ReLU/linear
#' decoder, returning logits. Names are decoder/W\[latent+classes,hidden\], decoder/b,
#' output/W\[hidden,features\], output/b, ordinary Glorot-uniform weights and zero
#' biases; these are the full model's same four decoder leaves. There is no image,
#' posterior, noise, RNG, prediction of labels or hidden fitting. The prior is
#' supplied by the caller and need not depend on class. Apply sigmoid for pixel
#' probabilities, which are not sampled Bernoulli pixels.
#'
#' Finite signed/zero nonempty Float32/64 codes must match store dtype. Integer
#' conditions \[B\] are validated before one-hot encoding or leaf creation; unlike
#' one_hot(), invalid classes never silently produce all-off rows. Strides and
#' continuous code/leaf gradients are retained; labels have no gradients. Inputs
#' unchanged; output owns lifetime. Incompatible leaves can fail after validation;
#' computed overflow is not repaired. Work O(B*hidden*(latent+classes+features));
#' shared stores require external synchronization.
#' @usage
#' ConditionalVaeDecode(parameters, code, labels, features = 784, classes = 10, latent = 2, hidden = 64)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable floating store containing/creating the decoder leaves.
#' @param code `Tensor`. Finite nonempty \[B,latent\] Float32/64 code matching store dtype.
#' @param labels `Tensor`. Required Int32/64 \[B\] supplied classes in \[0,classes).
#' @param features `integer`. Positive pixel width, default784.
#' @param classes `integer`. Positive one-hot width, default10.
#' @param latent `integer`. Positive code width, default2.
#' @param hidden `integer`. Positive decoder ReLU width, default64.
#' @return `Tensor`: Owning differentiable logits \[B,features\].
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(2111);
#' const auto labels=n::from_vector(std::vector<int>{3,8},{2});
#' const auto pixels=n::sigmoid(n::models::ConditionalVaeDecode(p,n::zeros({2,2}),labels));
#' // [2,784] probabilities conditioned on supplied classes3 and8, without images.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid inputs/options or incompatible leaves.
#' @references - Kingma and Welling (2013/2014), Auto-Encoding Variational Bayes,
#'   https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.6114 . Uses a fixed standard-normal prior.
#' - Sohn, Lee and Yan (2015), Learning Structured Output Representation using
#'   Deep Conditional Generative Models,
#'   https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2015/hash/8d55a249e6baa5c06772297520da2051-Abstract.html
#'   . This teaching variant uses supplied digit classes and a fixed shared prior, not the paper's
#'   learned conditional-prior structured-output architecture.
#' @seealso ConditionalVariationalAutoencoder(), GaussianVaeDecode()
#' @md
#'
`ConditionalVaeDecode` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1033`, NULL, list(...))
#' Evaluate a class-conditioned Gaussian/Bernoulli VAE with explicit posterior noise.
#'
#' Conditions BOTH q(z|image,class) and p(image|z,class): image then one-hot class
#' -> hidden ReLU -> separate linear mu/log_variance heads; z=mu+exp(lv/2)*epsilon;
#' z then the same class -> hidden ReLU -> pixel logits. The prior is shared
#' N(0,I), not learned or class-specific. Names/order: encoder/W,b; mu/W,b;
#' log_variance/W,b; decoder/W,b; output/W,b. Ordinary Glorot weights/zero biases
#' give10 leaves/102932 default scalars. Bernoulli NLL sums features, Gaussian KL
#' sums latent coordinates, then images are averaged: loss=mean(NLL+KL), beta1.
#' Binary pixels give a one-draw negative ELBO estimate, not exact conditional
#' log likelihood. Soft \[0,1\] targets give Bernoulli cross-entropy rather than
#' a likelihood density for continuous-valued observations.
#' No label prediction, KL annealing, random draw, fitting or selection is hidden.
#'
#' Finite unit-range images and finite epsilon match store Float32/64. Conditions
#' are one Int32/64 index per image and are checked before leaves are created.
#' Continuous image/epsilon/leaf paths and strides are preserved. Returned fields
#' own lifetime; inputs unchanged. Incompatible stored leaves/nonfinite computed
#' states may fail later; overflow is not repaired. Work
#' O(B*hidden*(features+classes+latent)), storage O(B*(features+classes+hidden+latent)).
#' Shared stores require synchronization. Supplied classes remain required at inference.
#' @usage
#' ConditionalVariationalAutoencoder(parameters, image, labels, epsilon, features = 784, classes = 10, latent = 2, hidden = 64)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable floating parameter store.
#' @param image `Tensor`. Finite nonempty unit-range \[B,features\] image rows.
#' @param labels `Tensor`. Required Int32/64 \[B\] supplied classes in \[0,classes).
#' @param epsilon `Tensor`. Explicit finite \[B,latent\] standard-normal noise, computation dtype.
#' @param features `integer`. Positive pixel width, default784.
#' @param classes `integer`. Positive one-hot width, default10.
#' @param latent `integer`. Positive Gaussian code width, default2.
#' @param hidden `integer`. Positive encoder/decoder ReLU width, default64.
#' @return `ConditionalVaeOutput`: Owning twelve-field ConditionalVaeOutput, including both concatenation endpoints.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(2111);
#' const auto out=n::models::ConditionalVariationalAutoencoder(p,n::full({2,784},.2),
#'     n::from_vector(std::vector<int>{0,9},{2}),n::zeros({2,2}));
#' const auto gradients=n::grad(out.loss,p.parameters()); //10 gradients; caller owns updates.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid input/options or incompatible stored leaves;
#'   nonfinite computed states may fail validation later.
#' @references - Kingma and Welling (2013/2014), https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.6114 .
#' - Sohn, Lee and Yan (2015),
#'   https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2015/hash/8d55a249e6baa5c06772297520da2051-Abstract.html
#'   .
#'   This small class-conditioned teaching model fixes the shared prior to N(0,I).
#' @seealso ConditionalVaeOutput, ConditionalVaeDecode(), GaussianReparameterize(), DiagonalGaussianKl(), BernoulliPixelNll()
#' @md
#'
`ConditionalVariationalAutoencoder` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1034`, NULL, list(...))
#' Evaluate the joint/product-marginal log-density ratio of an equally weighted Gaussian mixture.
#'
#' Uses ALL R supplied diagonal-Gaussian reference components, with stable
#' logsumexp over component log densities. Joint: logsumexp_r(sum_d log q_rd)-log R;
#' product: sum_d(logsumexp_r(log q_rd)-log R). This is exact finite empirical
#' density evaluation, not the paper's minibatch-weighted estimator. Expectations
#' remain Monte Carlo when z is sampled; pointwise/finite-mean estimates can be
#' negative. A single diagonal component or one latent coordinate factorizes.
#' References must be fitting-only when used for training/evaluation selection;
#' the function cannot infer corpus membership. Both reference heads stay LIVE,
#' including reference encoder parameter paths; no detach/RNG/clipping is hidden.
#'
#' Inputs are finite nonempty Float32/64 matrices with matching dtype/latent
#' width; reference means/log variances share \[R,L\], samples may have B!=R.
#' Strides and all three continuous gradients are retained; inputs unchanged,
#' output owns lifetime. Float32 log(2*pi) and log R are rounded to Float32 before
#' graph arithmetic, matching the source; Float64 uses full double constants.
#' Work/storage O(B*R*L). Exp/square overflow or all-minus-infinity logsumexp
#' states are not repaired. No shared mutable state.
#' @usage
#' EmpiricalTotalCorrelation(z, mu, log_variance)
#' @param z `Tensor`. Finite floating samples \[B,L\], B,L>0.
#' @param mu `Tensor`. Finite matching-dtype reference means \[R,L\], R>0.
#' @param log_variance `Tensor`. Finite matching-shape/dtype reference log variances \[R,L\].
#' @return `EmpiricalTcOutput`: Owning four-field empirical log-density ratio and mean.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' auto z=n::tensor({.3,-.2,1.,.4},{2,2}).requires_grad_();
#' auto mu=n::tensor({0.,0.,1.,-.3,-.5,.8},{3,2}).requires_grad_();
#' auto lv=n::zeros({3,2}).requires_grad_();
#' const auto out=n::models::EmpiricalTotalCorrelation(z,mu,lv);
#' const auto gradients=n::grad(out.mean,{z,mu,lv}); // all three paths, including references.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid input shape, dtype or values.
#' @references - Chen, Li, Grosse and Duvenaud (2018), Isolating Sources of Disentanglement in
#'   Variational Autoencoders, https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.04942 . This variant uses
#'   a complete equally weighted finite reference mixture, not its minibatch estimator.
#' @seealso BetaTcVariationalAutoencoder(), EmpiricalTcOutput, logsumexp()
#' @md
#'
`EmpiricalTotalCorrelation` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1035`, NULL, list(...))
#' Evaluate a tanh/linear density-ratio discriminator, with positive logit meaning joint.
#'
#' Uses hidden/W\[latent,hidden\], hidden/b\[hidden\], ratio/W\[hidden,1\],ratio/b\[1\],
#' ordinary Glorot-uniform weights and zero biases: four default leaves/97 scalars.
#' Negative logit means product of marginals. No probability, labels, detach,
#' RNG or training is hidden; all code/store derivatives remain. Finite signed/zero
#' strided Float32/64 rows match store dtype. Validation precedes leaf creation;
#' incompatible leaves can fail later, computed nonfinite states are not sanitized.
#' Output owns lifetime; inputs unchanged. Work O(B*latent*hidden); synchronize stores.
#' @usage
#' FactorDiscriminator(discriminator, z, latent = 4, hidden = 16)
#' @param discriminator `ParameterStore`. Mutable floating store, normally separate from the VAE.
#' @param z `Tensor`. Finite nonempty code rows \[B,latent\], matching store dtype.
#' @param latent `integer`. Positive code width, default4.
#' @param hidden `integer`. Positive tanh width, default16.
#' @return `Tensor`: Owning differentiable logits \[B\], not probabilities.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' dm::nn::ParameterStore d(2309);
#' const auto logits=dm::nn::models::FactorDiscriminator(d,dm::nn::zeros({3,4}));
#' // [3] logits; four leaves/97 scalars with hidden and ratio prefixes.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid width/input/dtype or incompatible leaves.
#' @references - Kim and Mnih (2018), Disentangling by Factorising,
#'   https://proceedings.mlr.press/v80/kim18b.html . Small tanh teaching discriminator.
#' @seealso FactorDiscriminatorLoss(), FactorVariationalAutoencoder()
#' @md
#'
`FactorDiscriminator` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1036`, NULL, list(...))
#' Average balanced joint-versus-product softplus cross-entropy for FactorVAE's discriminator.
#'
#' Returns0.5*(mean softplus(-D(joint))+mean softplus(D(product))). Each batch
#' is averaged separately, so row counts can differ. Joint has label1/product0;
#' the factor0.5 matches the source's equal-prior binary classification objective.
#' Inputs/store retain all mathematical gradients; the caller MUST detach posterior
#' samples and request/update only D when implementing a discriminator-only phase.
#' No optimizer, detachment, sampling or permuting is hidden. Matching finite
#' Float32/64 matrices and both positive widths are checked before leaf creation.
#' Owning scalar; inputs unchanged. Same numerical/store synchronization limits
#' and per-row work as FactorDiscriminator().
#' @usage
#' FactorDiscriminatorLoss(discriminator, joint, product, latent = 4, hidden = 16)
#' @param discriminator `ParameterStore`. Mutable floating discriminator store.
#' @param joint `Tensor`. Finite nonempty joint rows \[B,latent\], matching dtype.
#' @param product `Tensor`. Finite nonempty product rows \[P,latent\], P may differ from B.
#' @param latent `integer`. Positive code width, default4.
#' @param hidden `integer`. Positive tanh width, default16.
#' @return `Tensor`: Owning differentiable scalar cross-entropy, in nats.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore d(2309);
#' const auto loss=n::models::FactorDiscriminatorLoss(d,n::zeros({2,4}),n::ones({3,4}));
#' const auto gradients=n::grad(loss,d.parameters()); // caller decides when to update D.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid input/options or incompatible leaves.
#' @references - Kim and Mnih (2018), https://proceedings.mlr.press/v80/kim18b.html .
#' @seealso FactorDiscriminator(), PermuteLatentColumns()
#' @md
#'
`FactorDiscriminatorLoss` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1037`, NULL, list(...))
#' Independently shuffle each latent column with a local seeded MT19937 stream.
#'
#' Resets row indices for each column and calls std::shuffle on one local stream,
#' preserving each empirical marginal exactly while breaking row alignment. Output
#' is a DETACHED owning same-shape/dtype Float32/64 matrix; inputs unchanged. There
#' is no global RNG/state, optimizer or gradient through the discrete permutation.
#' Reproducible for matching seed, data and C++ standard-library shuffle implementation;
#' the standard does not promise identical shuffle sequences across libraries.
#' Finite nonempty strided rows are accepted. Work/storage O(B*L). Finite shuffled
#' batches are a product-marginal surrogate, not independent population draws.
#' @usage
#' PermuteLatentColumns(joint, seed)
#' @param joint `Tensor`. Finite nonempty \[B,L\] Float32/64 joint samples.
#' @param seed `integer`. Explicit uint32 seed for the local shuffle stream.
#' @return `Tensor`: Detached owning shuffled \[B,L\], preserving all column value bytes.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' const auto x=n::tensor({1.,10.,2.,20.,3.,30.},{3,2}).requires_grad_();
#' const auto product=n::models::PermuteLatentColumns(x,2311);
#' // Each column's multiset is unchanged; product.requires_grad() is false.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid shape, dtype or nonfinite values.
#' @references - Kim and Mnih (2018), https://proceedings.mlr.press/v80/kim18b.html , empirical
#'   product-of-marginals construction via independent within-batch permutations.
#' @seealso FactorDiscriminatorLoss()
#' @md
#'
`PermuteLatentColumns` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1038`, NULL, list(...))
#' Evaluate FactorVAE's Gaussian model and discriminator-logit TC surrogate with explicit noise.
#'
#' Composes BetaVariationalAutoencoder(beta1) with mean FactorDiscriminator(z).
#' loss=mean(summed-pixel NLL+summed-latent analytic KL)+extra_weight*tc. Default
#' gamma3 gives51416 VAE scalars/ten leaves plus97 discriminator scalars/four leaves.
#' Positive logit means joint; this surrogate may be negative and does not certify
#' true TC or disentanglement. No detach is hidden: VAE gradients pass through D
#' to z while the caller requests/updates VAE leaves only. D's separate phase must
#' detach fresh posterior samples, construct product rows and update D separately.
#' Distinct same-dtype stores, finite unit-range image and explicit noise, positive
#' widths and representable nonnegative weight are checked before any creation.
#' Incompatible leaves/nonfinite computed states may fail later. Owning fields;
#' inputs unchanged. Same continuous-gradient/overflow/soft-target semantics as
#' BetaVariationalAutoencoder(); no RNG, fitting, annealing or checkpoint selection.
#' Work is the VAE plus O(B*latent*disc_hidden); shared stores need synchronization.
#' @usage
#' FactorVariationalAutoencoder(parameters, discriminator, image, epsilon, extra_weight = 3.0, features = 784, latent = 4, hidden = 32, disc_hidden = 16)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable floating VAE store, distinct from discriminator.
#' @param discriminator `ParameterStore`. Mutable matching-dtype store, distinct from parameters.
#' @param image `Tensor`. Finite nonempty unit-range \[B,features\] pixels.
#' @param epsilon `Tensor`. Explicit finite same-dtype/batch \[B,latent\] Gaussian noise.
#' @param extra_weight `numeric`. Finite nonnegative gamma, default3;0 is an ablation.
#' @param features `integer`. Positive image width, default784.
#' @param latent `integer`. Positive code width, default4.
#' @param hidden `integer`. Positive VAE ReLU width, default32.
#' @param disc_hidden `integer`. Positive discriminator tanh width, default16.
#' @return `TcVaeOutput`: Owning TcVaeOutput; base net losses are not overwritten.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(2237),d(2309);
#' const auto out=n::models::FactorVariationalAutoencoder(p,d,n::full({2,784},.2),n::zeros({2,4}));
#' const auto vae_gradients=n::grad(out.loss,p.parameters());
#' // Fixed D still propagates gradients to z.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid inputs/options, aliased/mismatched stores or
#'   incompatible leaves.
#' @references - Kim and Mnih (2018), Disentangling by Factorising,
#'   https://proceedings.mlr.press/v80/kim18b.html . This uses the migrated small
#'   ReLU/Bernoulli VAE and tanh discriminator, not the paper's benchmark network.
#' @seealso TcVaeOutput, FactorDiscriminatorLoss(), BetaVariationalAutoencoder()
#' @md
#'
`FactorVariationalAutoencoder` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1039`, NULL, list(...))
#' Evaluate betaTCVAE with all supplied fitting posteriors as live finite-mixture references.
#'
#' Base model is BetaVariationalAutoencoder(beta1). Reuses its encoder/mu/
#' log_variance leaves on reference_images, preserving ALL reference encoder
#' gradients, then evaluates EmpiricalTotalCorrelation on sampled z. No reference
#' decoder or random draw is computed. loss=base NLL+analytic KL+extra_weight*tc;
#' default extra3 corresponds to beta_TC4, leaving other decomposition weights1.
#' This complete equally weighted finite mixture is an educational adaptation,
#' not the original minibatch-weighted density estimator. It is exact as a finite
#' density; supplied z only estimates its expected TC, so negatives are retained.
#'
#' reference_images MUST contain fitting rows only, never validation/test; this
#' is caller-owned provenance. Matching finite unit-range matrices, explicit noise,
#' positive widths and representable nonnegative weight are validated before
#' creation. Inputs unchanged; all fields own lifetime, with sample/reference
#' input/encoder paths live. Incompatible leaves/nonfinite arithmetic may fail
#' later. No implicit detach, sampling, training or selection. Work/storage
#' includes O(B*R*latent), with no hidden reference chunking. Synchronize stores.
#' @usage
#' BetaTcVariationalAutoencoder(parameters, image, epsilon, reference_images, extra_weight = 3.0, features = 784, latent = 4, hidden = 32)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable floating VAE store shared by sample/reference encoders.
#' @param image `Tensor`. Finite nonempty \[B,features\] unit-range pixels.
#' @param epsilon `Tensor`. Explicit finite matching-dtype/batch \[B,latent\] Gaussian noise.
#' @param reference_images `Tensor`. Finite nonempty \[R,features\] fitting-only pixel rows, computation dtype.
#' @param extra_weight `numeric`. Finite nonnegative beta_TC-1, default3.
#' @param features `integer`. Positive image width, default784.
#' @param latent `integer`. Positive code width, default4.
#' @param hidden `integer`. Positive encoder/decoder ReLU width, default32.
#' @return `TcVaeOutput`: Owning TcVaeOutput with base losses and a signed exact-mixture TC sample mean.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(2237);
#' const auto
#' out=n::models::BetaTcVariationalAutoencoder(p,n::full({2,784},.2),n::zeros({2,4}),n::full({3,784},.3));
#' const auto gradients=n::grad(out.loss,p.parameters()); // Includes fitting-reference head paths.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid inputs/options/reference rows or incompatible leaves.
#' @references - Chen, Li, Grosse and Duvenaud (2018), Isolating Sources of Disentanglement in
#'   Variational Autoencoders, https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.04942 . Complete finite
#'   reference mixture replaces its minibatch estimator; no MIG claim is made.
#' @seealso EmpiricalTotalCorrelation(), TcVaeOutput, BetaVariationalAutoencoder()
#' @md
#'
`BetaTcVariationalAutoencoder` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1040`, NULL, list(...))
#' Apply or invert a context-conditioned affine coupling, preserving the unchanged half.
#'
#' Split code into equal halves; swap=false conditions on the first and transforms
#' the second, swap=true conditions on the second and transforms the first.
#' Concatenate the unchanged half with supplied context\[C\], then use
#' name/conditioner: (L/2+C)->hidden ReLU and name/heads: hidden->L linear.
#' Heads give scale=0.5*tanh(raw_scale) and shift, each L/2 wide. Forward computes
#' b*exp(scale)+shift and sum(scale); inverse computes (b-shift)*exp(-scale)
#' and -sum(scale). Coordinate ordering is preserved, not swapped on output.
#' The Jacobian is triangular in split coordinates with context fixed; each
#' row's absolute logdet is bounded by L/4. Computed exp overflow is not repaired.
#'
#' Finite nonempty matching Float32/64 matrices preserve strides and all continuous
#' gradients. Positive even latent width and positive context/hidden widths are
#' checked before leaf creation. Weights use ordinary Glorot uniform, biases zero.
#' No RNG draws beyond lazy parameter initialization, detach, fit or optimizer is
#' hidden. Inputs unchanged; returned handles own lifetime. Synchronize shared
#' stores. Work O(B*hidden*(L+C)); incompatible stored leaves can fail later.
#' @usage
#' ConditionalAffinePosteriorFlow(parameters, name, code, context, swap = FALSE, inverse = FALSE, latent = 4, hidden = 16)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable matching-dtype floating store, with shared forward/inverse leaves.
#' @param name `character`. Nonempty parameter prefix; reuse exactly for inversion, e.g. flow0.
#' @param code `Tensor`. Finite code \[B,latent\], B>0, with store dtype.
#' @param context `Tensor`. Finite same-batch/dtype conditioning rows \[B,C\], C>0.
#' @param swap `logical`. Condition on the second half when true; default false.
#' @param inverse `logical`. Invert the same conditioned mapping when true; default false.
#' @param latent `integer`. Positive even code width, default4.
#' @param hidden `integer`. Positive conditioner ReLU width, default16.
#' @return `AffinePosteriorFlowOutput`: Owning transformed code, signed logdet, forward scale and shift.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(3049);
#' const auto z=n::ones({2,4}),c=n::zeros({2,2});
#' const auto f=n::models::ConditionalAffinePosteriorFlow(p,"f",z,c);
#' const auto back=n::models::ConditionalAffinePosteriorFlow(p,"f",f.value,c,false,true);
#' // back.value recovers z up to roundoff; log determinants cancel.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid inputs/options, width overflow or incompatible leaves.
#' @references - Dinh, Sohl-Dickstein and Bengio (2016/2017), Density estimation using Real NVP,
#'   https://arxiv.org/abs/1605.08803 . Uses affine coupling with an added external
#'   context and bounded log scales, not an image-space Real NVP generative model.
#' @seealso AffinePosteriorFlowOutput, FlowVariationalAutoencoder()
#' @md
#'
`ConditionalAffinePosteriorFlow` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1041`, NULL, list(...))
#' Evaluate a Gaussian VAE with two alternating conditional affine posterior couplings.
#'
#' Encoder features->hidden ReLU feeds two latent-wide linear Gaussian heads.
#' Explicit epsilon samples z0; two ConditionalAffinePosteriorFlow calls use that
#' same live image context, first with swap=false, then true. Only final z enters
#' GaussianVaeDecode; no deterministic context bypass exists. Forward/inverse
#' share names/parameters; weights are ordinary Glorot uniform, biases zero.
#' Default architecture has eighteen leaves/52672 scalars, including1256 flow
#' scalars; creation order is encoder,mu,log_variance,flow0,flow1,decoder,output.
#'
#' logq=logq0-SUM(forward logdet); logp is standard normal at final z. Train
#' mean(summed pixel NLL+logq-logp). This sampled ratio can be negative and is
#' not clipped or replaced by base analytic Gaussian KL. Float32 uses the original
#' float-rounded log(2*pi), Float64 uses full precision. Epsilon-zero decoding
#' yields decode(f(mu)); nonlinear f(mu) is not generally the flowed mean.
#' Prior sampling uses GaussianVaeDecode directly on standard-normal FINAL codes,
#' without image, context or posterior flow. This is a flexible inference family,
#' not an image-space exact-likelihood flow or a learned flow prior.
#'
#' Validates finite unit-range nonempty image and matching finite explicit noise,
#' positive widths/even latent and addition overflow before creating leaves.
#' Strides and all image/noise/context/parameter gradients stay live. No fitting,
#' RNG draws beyond lazy initialization, detach or checkpoint selection is hidden.
#' Computed nonfinite values are not sanitized; incompatible leaves may fail later.
#' Inputs unchanged; all outputs own lifetime. Shared stores require synchronization.
#' Work includes O(B*hidden*(features+latent)+B*flow_hidden*(latent+hidden));
#' inverse diagnostics evaluate the shared conditioners again without extra leaves.
#' @usage
#' FlowVariationalAutoencoder(parameters, image, epsilon, features = 784, latent = 4, hidden = 32, flow_hidden = 16)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable matching floating store; normally seed3001 for this experiment.
#' @param image `Tensor`. Finite Float32/64 pixel rows \[B,features\], B>0, in \[0,1\].
#' @param epsilon `Tensor`. Finite same-dtype/batch explicit standard-normal rows \[B,latent\].
#' @param features `integer`. Positive pixel width, default784.
#' @param latent `integer`. Positive even code width, default4.
#' @param hidden `integer`. Positive encoder-context/decoder width, default32.
#' @param flow_hidden `integer`. Positive coupling-conditioner width, default16.
#' @return `FlowVaeOutput`: Owning eighteen-field flow VAE output with sampled scalar negative ELBO.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(3001);
#' const auto out=n::models::FlowVariationalAutoencoder(
#'     p,n::full({2,784},.2),n::ones({2,4}));
#' const auto gradients=n::grad(out.loss,p.parameters());
#' // Caller owns the optimizer and updates; all eighteen leaves receive derivatives.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid inputs/options, overflow or incompatible leaves.
#' @references - Rezende and Mohamed (2015), Variational Inference with Normalizing Flows,
#'   https://proceedings.mlr.press/v37/rezende15.html . Uses posterior change of variables.
#' - Dinh, Sohl-Dickstein and Bengio (2016/2017), https://arxiv.org/abs/1605.08803 .
#'   Uses two small context-conditioned affine couplings, not the paper's benchmark network.
#' @seealso FlowVaeOutput, ConditionalAffinePosteriorFlow(), GaussianVaeDecode()
#' @md
#'
`FlowVariationalAutoencoder` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1042`, NULL, list(...))
#' Reparameterize an explicit Gumbel draw with softmax and retain its hard categorical sample.
#'
#' q=exp(log_softmax(logits)), y=softmax((logits+noise)/temperature), and
#' hard=argmax(logits+noise). Hard samples ignore positive temperature; relaxed
#' values and gradients do not. No straight-through estimator is used. Both
#' logits and noise receive relaxed-path derivatives; q does not depend on noise.
#' Inputs are finite nonempty matching Float32/64 matrices with C>=2. Row counts
#' B,M must match or one is1, supporting one categorical distribution with many
#' noise draws. Strides/broadcast gradients are retained; inputs unchanged.
#' Temperature is rounded to Float32 before division for Float32 inputs, matching
#' the original experiment; invalid/unrepresentable values are rejected. Computed
#' score/division overflow is not repaired. No RNG, fitting, clipping or shared
#' state; output handles own lifetime. Work/storage O(max(B,M)*C).
#' @usage
#' GumbelCategorical(logits, noise, temperature = 0.69999999999999996)
#' @param logits `Tensor`. Finite floating unnormalized log probabilities \[B,C\].
#' @param noise `Tensor`. Finite same-dtype explicit Gumbel values \[M,C\], compatible rows.
#' @param temperature `numeric`. Finite representable positive scalar, default .7 (narrowed for Float32).
#' @return `GumbelCategoricalSample`: Owning categorical sample; posterior fields keep B rows, draw fields use broadcast rows.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' auto logits=n::zeros({1,3}).requires_grad_();
#' const auto sample=n::models::GumbelCategorical(logits,n::tensor({.1,.4,-.3},{1,3}));
#' const auto gradients=n::grad(n::sum(n::square(sample.relaxed)),{logits});
#' // Hard index1; no hard argmax derivative is inserted into fitting.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid shapes/dtypes/values or temperature.
#' @references - Jang, Gu and Poole (2016/2017), Categorical Reparameterization with Gumbel-Softmax,
#'   https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.01144 . Uses the soft path, without straight-through gradients.
#' - Maddison, Mnih and Teh (2016/2017), The Concrete Distribution: A Continuous
#'   Relaxation of Discrete Random Variables, https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.00712 .
#' @seealso GumbelNoise(), CategoricalUniformKl(), GumbelCategoricalAutoencoder()
#' @md
#'
`GumbelCategorical` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1043`, NULL, list(...))
#' Sum categorical KL to a uniform distribution over each row's classes.
#'
#' Returns sum(q*(logq+log C),axis=1), without sampling or averaging classes.
#' Float32 rounds log C to Float32 before addition; Float64 uses full precision.
#' q and logq must be finite nonempty matching Float32/64 \[B,C\], C>=2, with
#' q values in\[0,1\]. The caller supplies normalized q and its matching logq;
#' that relationship is not checked, allowing independent derivatives of both
#' arguments. Stable finite log-softmax values allow q=0 from underflow without
#' evaluating log(0). Negative roundoff is retained, not clipped. Inputs unchanged;
#' all continuous paths/strides and owning lifetime retained. O(B*C), no shared state.
#' @usage
#' CategoricalUniformKl(probability, log_probability)
#' @param probability `Tensor`. Finite unit-range categorical probabilities \[B,C\].
#' @param log_probability `Tensor`. Finite same-shape/dtype log probabilities \[B,C\].
#' @return `Tensor`: Owning differentiable per-row KL \[B\], nats, when supplied probabilities are consistent.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' const auto s=n::models::GumbelCategorical(n::zeros({2,3}),n::zeros({2,3}));
#' const auto kl=n::models::CategoricalUniformKl(s.probability,s.log_probability);
#' // Uniform posterior: each row's KL is0, up to roundoff.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid shapes, dtype or values.
#' @references - Jang, Gu and Poole (2016/2017), https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.01144 . This is
#'   the discrete categorical KL, not a KL between continuous Concrete densities.
#' @seealso GumbelCategorical(), GumbelCategoricalAutoencoder()
#' @md
#'
`CategoricalUniformKl` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1044`, NULL, list(...))
#' Generate explicit Gumbel noise from a local seeded MT19937 midpoint-uniform stream.
#'
#' Each uint32 engine draw r gives u=(double(r)+.5)/2^32 and g=-log(-log(u)).
#' No normal/distribution-library sampler or global RNG is involved. Saves both
#' Float64 intermediates and the exact Float32 cast, without endpoint clipping.
#' Positive dimensions/C>=2 and element/byte-count overflow are checked before
#' allocation. Outputs own lifetime; no inputs or shared state are mutated.
#' Work/storage O(samples*n*groups*classes). Reproducibility assumes matching
#' floating log implementations; engine values are prescribed MT19937 outputs.
#' @usage
#' GumbelNoise(n, samples, seed, groups = 4, classes = 8)
#' @param n `integer`. Positive rows per draw.
#' @param samples `integer`. Positive independent draw count.
#' @param seed `integer`. Full unsigned32 local engine seed.
#' @param groups `integer`. Positive categorical-variable count, default4.
#' @param classes `integer`. At least two classes per variable, default8.
#' @return `GumbelNoiseBank`: Three detached owning banks of shape\[samples,n,groups,classes\].
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' const auto bank=dm::nn::models::GumbelNoise(5,2,2969,1,3);
#' const auto draw=dm::nn::select(bank.noise,0,0).contiguous();
#' // draw has shape[5,1,3]; the model never samples implicitly.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid dimensions or element/byte-count overflow.
#' @references - Jang, Gu and Poole (2016/2017), https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.01144 , Gumbel transform.
#' - Maddison, Mnih and Teh (2016/2017), https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.00712 .
#' @seealso GumbelNoiseBank, GumbelCategorical()
#' @md
#'
`GumbelNoise` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1045`, NULL, list(...))
#' Decode supplied grouped simplex codes into Bernoulli probabilities without image targets.
#'
#' Flattens groups/classes to one code axis and uses GaussianVaeDecode's shared
#' decoder/output names, with ordinary Glorot weights and zero biases, followed
#' by sigmoid. Each group sums to1 within strict2e-6(Float32)/1e-12(Float64).
#' All values are finite and in\[0,1\]. Validation precedes leaf creation; incompatible
#' stored leaves/nonfinite computed states may fail later. Inputs unchanged;
#' outputs own lifetime and all continuous code/parameter gradients, including
#' strided inputs. No RNG, fitting or no-grad scope is hidden. Synchronize stores.
#' Work O(B*hidden*(groups*classes+features)); default four leaves/26928 scalars.
#' @usage
#' GumbelDecodeSoft(parameters, simplex, features = 784, groups = 4, classes = 8, hidden = 32)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable matching-dtype floating decoder store.
#' @param simplex `Tensor`. Nonempty matching floating code\[B,groups,classes\].
#' @param features `integer`. Positive output pixel width, default784.
#' @param groups `integer`. Positive categorical-variable count, default4.
#' @param classes `integer`. At least two categories, default8, fitting Int32 model IDs.
#' @param hidden `integer`. Positive decoder ReLU width, default32.
#' @return `Tensor`: Owning differentiable Bernoulli probabilities\[B,features\], not sampled pixels.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(2903);
#' const auto probability=n::models::GumbelDecodeSoft(p,n::full({2,4,8},.125));
#' // [2,784] output; target images and an encoder are unnecessary.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid code/options/overflow or incompatible leaves.
#' @seealso GumbelDecodeIds(), GumbelCategoricalAutoencoder(), GaussianVaeDecode()
#' @md
#'
`GumbelDecodeSoft` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1046`, NULL, list(...))
#' Decode hard categorical IDs through the same target-free Bernoulli decoder as soft codes.
#'
#' Accepts finite in-range Int32/Int64 IDs; constructs same-store-dtype one-hot
#' codes then calls GumbelDecodeSoft. IDs are discrete; decoder parameters retain
#' gradients. No encoder/image/RNG/fitting is used; input unchanged, output owns
#' lifetime. Validation precedes leaf creation; incompatible leaves may fail later.
#' Strides accepted. Work includes O(B*groups*classes) one-hot storage; synchronize stores.
#' @usage
#' GumbelDecodeIds(parameters, ids, features = 784, groups = 4, classes = 8, hidden = 32)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable Float32/64 decoder store.
#' @param ids `Tensor`. Nonempty Int32/64 category IDs\[B,groups\], each in\[0,classes).
#' @param features `integer`. Positive output pixel width, default784.
#' @param groups `integer`. Positive categorical-variable count, default4.
#' @param classes `integer`. At least two categories, default8, fitting Int32 model IDs.
#' @param hidden `integer`. Positive decoder ReLU width, default32.
#' @return `Tensor`: Owning differentiable Bernoulli probabilities\[B,features\].
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(2903);
#' const auto images=n::models::GumbelDecodeIds(p,n::from_vector(std::vector<int>{0,1,2,3},{1,4}));
#' // One [1,784] decoder-probability row, independent of a target image.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid IDs/options/dtype/overflow or incompatible leaves.
#' @seealso GumbelDecodeSoft(), GumbelCategoricalAutoencoder()
#' @md
#'
`GumbelDecodeIds` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1047`, NULL, list(...))
#' Evaluate a categorical VAE trained by a soft Gumbel reconstruction plus discrete KL surrogate.
#'
#' Encoder features->hidden ReLU->groups*classes logits; independent Gumbel-softmax
#' draws form flattened relaxed codes. One shared ReLU/linear Bernoulli decoder
#' evaluates both relaxed and matched one-hot codes. Parameters are encoder/W,b;
#' categorical/W,b;decoder/W,b;output/W,b, created in that order with ordinary
#' Glorot-uniform weights and zero biases. Defaults are four8-way variables,
#' hidden32,784 pixels and eight leaves/53104 scalars, seed2903 in the report.
#'
#' loss=mean(sum_pixels soft NLL+sum_groups discrete KL(q||Uniform(C))). This mixes
#' a relaxed likelihood and a discrete KL: it is a biased continuous surrogate,
#' not an exact discrete ELBO or Concrete-density KL. hard_objective instead uses
#' actual Gumbel-max categorical draws, yielding a discrete Monte Carlo negative
#' ELBO diagnostic. Training inserts no straight-through derivative. Temperature
#' affects soft values/gradients only, not q, discrete KL or hard sample indices.
#' Float32 narrows temperature and log C to preserve the original scalar arithmetic.
#'
#' Unit-range finite nonempty images\[B,F\] and matching finite supplied noise\[B,G,C\]
#' are validated with positive widths/C>=2 and multiplication overflow before
#' creating leaves. No RNG, fitting, gradient request, optimizer or checkpoint
#' selection is hidden. Continuous image/noise/parameter paths stay live; hard
#' argmax paths do not. Soft-target NLL is cross-entropy rather than binary NLL.
#' Computed nonfinite states may fail later; no clipping/repair. Inputs unchanged,
#' owning results/strides preserved; synchronize stores. Work includes
#' O(B*hidden*(features+groups*classes)); the hard decoder is evaluated separately.
#' @usage
#' GumbelCategoricalAutoencoder(parameters, image, noise, temperature = 0.69999999999999996, features = 784, groups = 4, classes = 8, hidden = 32)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable matching Float32/64 parameter store.
#' @param image `Tensor`. Finite pixel rows\[B,features\], B>0, in\[0,1\].
#' @param noise `Tensor`. Finite matching-dtype explicit Gumbels\[B,groups,classes\].
#' @param temperature `numeric`. Positive representable scalar, default .7 (narrowed for Float32); no annealing.
#' @param features `integer`. Positive image width, default784.
#' @param groups `integer`. Positive number of categorical variables, default4.
#' @param classes `integer`. At least two categories, default8, fitting Int32 model IDs.
#' @param hidden `integer`. Positive encoder/decoder ReLU width, default32.
#' @return `GumbelCategoricalAutoencoderOutput`: Owning fourteen-field forward output with soft loss and hard diagnostic.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(2903);
#' const auto out=n::models::GumbelCategoricalAutoencoder(
#'     p,n::full({2,784},.2),n::zeros({2,4,8}));
#' const auto gradients=n::grad(out.loss,p.parameters());
#' // Caller owns Adam and fitting; all eight leaves receive soft-path/KL gradients.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid inputs/options/overflow or incompatible leaves.
#' @references - Jang, Gu and Poole (2016/2017), Categorical Reparameterization with Gumbel-Softmax,
#'   https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.01144 . Uses relaxed training, without straight-through gradients.
#' - Maddison, Mnih and Teh (2016/2017), The Concrete Distribution: A Continuous Relaxation
#'   of Discrete Random Variables, https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.00712 . This migrated
#'   small architecture is a teaching experiment, not a benchmark reproduction.
#' @seealso GumbelCategoricalAutoencoderOutput, GumbelCategorical(), CategoricalUniformKl(), GumbelDecodeSoft(), GumbelDecodeIds(), GumbelNoise()
#' @md
#'
`GumbelCategoricalAutoencoder` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1048`, NULL, list(...))
#' Construct direct Gaussian posterior heads or additive corrections to a learned prior.
#'
#' residual=true returns(prior_mu+head_mu,prior_lv+head_lv); false returns the
#' supplied head tensors directly. This is additive residual parameterization,
#' not precision-weighted evidence/prior fusion. All four matrices must be finite
#' nonempty matching Float32/64\[B,L\]. Strides and live paths are retained, with no
#' clipping, RNG or input mutation. Direct mode has no prior gradient path.
#' Results own lifetime; O(B*L) work/storage, no shared state. Arithmetic overflow
#' is not repaired. Zero residuals make posterior and prior identical.
#' @usage
#' HierarchyPosterior(prior_mu, prior_lv, head_mu, head_lv, residual = TRUE)
#' @param prior_mu `Tensor`. Finite conditional prior mean\[B,L\].
#' @param prior_lv `Tensor`. Finite matching conditional prior log variance\[B,L\].
#' @param head_mu `Tensor`. Finite matching direct mean or residual mean correction.
#' @param head_lv `Tensor`. Finite matching direct log variance or residual correction.
#' @param residual `logical`. Add corrections to the prior when true, default true.
#' @return `HierarchyGaussian`: Owning posterior mean and log variance, retaining applicable continuous paths.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' const auto q=n::models::HierarchyPosterior(n::ones({1,2}),n::zeros({1,2}),
#'     n::full({1,2},.2),n::full({1,2},.1));
#' // Posterior mean1.2 and log variance.1 in both coordinates.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid shapes/dtypes/values.
#' @references - Vahdat and Kautz (2020), NVAE: A Deep Hierarchical Variational Autoencoder,
#'   https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.03898 . Uses additive residual Gaussian parameters.
#' @seealso HierarchyGaussian, HierarchicalVariationalAutoencoder(), ConditionalGaussianKl()
#' @md
#'
`HierarchyPosterior` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1049`, NULL, list(...))
#' Sum analytic KL between two diagonal conditional Gaussians over latent coordinates.
#'
#' Computes0.5*sum((prior_lv-lv)+(exp(lv-prior_lv)+(mu-prior_mu)^2*exp(-prior_lv))-1,
#' axis1). All four finite nonempty matching Float32/64\[B,L\] matrices keep their
#' gradients and strides. The parenthesization preserves the migrated Float32
#' calculation. Identical inputs yield exact zero; negative roundoff is not clipped.
#' No sampling, coordinate averaging, variance clipping or shared state. Inputs
#' unchanged; output owns lifetime. O(B*L); computed exp overflow is not repaired.
#' @usage
#' ConditionalGaussianKl(mu, lv, prior_mu, prior_lv)
#' @param mu `Tensor`. Finite posterior mean\[B,L\].
#' @param lv `Tensor`. Finite matching posterior log variance\[B,L\].
#' @param prior_mu `Tensor`. Finite matching conditional prior mean\[B,L\].
#' @param prior_lv `Tensor`. Finite matching conditional prior log variance\[B,L\].
#' @return `Tensor`: Owning differentiable per-row conditional KL\[B\], nats.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' const auto kl=n::models::ConditionalGaussianKl(n::ones({2,3}),n::zeros({2,3}),
#'     n::zeros({2,3}),n::zeros({2,3}));
#' // KL is1.5 nats for each row.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid shapes/dtypes/values.
#' @references - Vahdat and Kautz (2020), https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.03898 , Gaussian hierarchy.
#' - Sonderby et al. (2016), Ladder Variational Autoencoders,
#'   https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.02282 , conditional Gaussian inference.
#' @seealso DiagonalGaussianKl(), HierarchyPosterior()
#' @md
#'
`ConditionalGaussianKl` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1050`, NULL, list(...))
#' Apply the miniature NVAE expansion/depthwise/BN/Swish/projection/SE residual cell.
#'
#' For C input channels: bias-free1x1 C->2C expansion, BN/Swish, depthwise3x3
#' multiplier1, BN/Swish, bias-free1x1 2C->C projection and BN. Spatial mean then
#' SE dense C->max(1,C/2) Swish->C sigmoid gates the project map. Returns
#' x+0.1*(project*gate). Names are cell/expand,expand_bn,depthwise,depth_bn,
#' project,project_bn,se_reduce,se_expand. Ordinary Glorot weights (depthwise
#' initialized per input channel), gamma1, beta0; default C4 gives13 leaves/
#' 198 scalars and six moving buffers/40 scalars. Use the same architecture per store.
#'
#' training=false uses detached moving buffers; true uses batch moments without
#' moving updates unless update_stats=true. Moving mean/UNBIASED variance use
#' momentum.9; normalization uses population variance plus.001. For one scalar
#' observation per channel the returned population variance is zero. Initial
#' calls lazily create parameters/buffers even in inference. Existing weights
#' never change; only explicit training/update passes change existing buffers.
#' Caller excludes state from gradients and optimizer; checkpoint both stores.
#' Validation precedes leaf creation; incompatible leaves/nonfinite computed
#' values may fail later. Inputs unchanged, all continuous paths own lifetime.
#' Shared stores require synchronization; no noise, optimizer or fitting is hidden.
#' @usage
#' HierarchyNvaeCell(parameters, state, x, training = FALSE, update_stats = FALSE)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable floating trainable store.
#' @param state `ParameterStore`. Separate same-dtype BN moving-statistics store.
#' @param x `Tensor`. Finite nonempty same-dtype NHWC\[B,H,W,C\] features, C>0 with2C representable.
#' @param training `logical`. Use batch moments when true, default false.
#' @param update_stats `logical`. Update moving moments when true; requires training, default false.
#' @return `Tensor`: Owning differentiable residual feature map with x's shape/dtype.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(2609),state(0);
#' const auto y=n::models::HierarchyNvaeCell(p,state,n::ones({2,7,7,4}),true,false);
#' // Batch moments are used; six moving buffers stay at their initial values.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid inputs/stores/flags, channel overflow or incompatible
#'   leaves.
#' @references - Vahdat and Kautz (2020), https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.03898 . This is one small
#'   cell with SE and residual scale.1, not the full deep/multiscale NVAE network.
#' @seealso HierarchicalVariationalAutoencoder(), batch_norm()
#' @md
#'
`HierarchyNvaeCell` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1051`, NULL, list(...))
#' Decode explicit lower and top latent samples without an image or posterior encoder.
#'
#' Projects the supplied top code to quarter-resolution features, applies the
#' selected ordinary/NVAE cell, combines the lower one-channel spatial code,
#' then repeat2/3x3 Swish/repeat2/3x3 logits and sigmoid. Both latent samples
#' are required; no image or encoder bypass. NVAE uses detached moving moments.
#' Same names/initializers as the full model, so restore BOTH parameter and BN
#' stores. Lazy inference can create missing leaves/buffers; existing values are
#' unchanged. Continuous code/parameter gradients stay live; no no-grad scope,
#' RNG, prior sampling or fitting is hidden. Inputs unchanged, output owns lifetime.
#' Validates geometry/stores/finite code rows before creation; incompatible leaves
#' or computed nonfinite states may fail later. Synchronize mutable stores.
#' @usage
#' HierarchyDecode(parameters, state, lower, top, nvae = FALSE, height = 28, width = 28, channels = 4)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable floating decoder/top-feature parameter store.
#' @param state `ParameterStore`. Separate matching-dtype NVAE statistics store, unused by ordinary hierarchy.
#' @param lower `Tensor`. Finite matching-dtype\[B,(height/4)*(width/4)\] lower sample.
#' @param top `Tensor`. Finite matching-dtype\[B,T\] top sample, T>0.
#' @param nvae `logical`. Select miniature NVAE cell when true, default false.
#' @param height `integer`. Positive multiple-of-four output height, default28.
#' @param width `integer`. Positive multiple-of-four output width, default28.
#' @param channels `integer`. Positive feature width, default4.
#' @return `Tensor`: Owning differentiable Bernoulli probabilities\[B,height*width\].
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(2609),state(0);
#' const auto probability=n::models::HierarchyDecode(p,state,n::zeros({2,49}),n::zeros({2,2}));
#' // Decoder-only[2,784] probabilities; neither image nor encoder is needed.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid inputs/geometry/stores or incompatible leaves.
#' @seealso HierarchyPrior(), HierarchicalVariationalAutoencoder()
#' @md
#'
`HierarchyDecode` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1052`, NULL, list(...))
#' Evaluate an ancestral prior draw using explicit top samples and conditional lower noise.
#'
#' For supplied top z2, evaluate learned p(z1|z2), sample its lower Gaussian with
#' the supplied epsilon, then decode BOTH latent groups. No image/encoder input,
#' posterior substitution, RNG or fitting is used. The NVAE cell uses moving
#' statistics, detached from autograd; restore BOTH stores for trained inference.
#' Initial calls may create missing leaves/buffers; existing values are unchanged.
#' All continuous top/noise/parameter paths and strides are retained; outputs own
#' lifetime. Validates matching nonempty floating rows and geometry/stores before
#' creation; computed nonfinite values/incompatible leaves may fail later.
#' @usage
#' HierarchyPrior(parameters, state, top, lower_noise, nvae = FALSE, height = 28, width = 28, channels = 4)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable matching floating generative parameter store.
#' @param state `ParameterStore`. Separate matching-dtype moving-statistics store, unused for ordinary hierarchy.
#' @param top `Tensor`. Finite\[B,T\] supplied top samples, T>0; normally independent N(0,I).
#' @param lower_noise `Tensor`. Finite same-batch/dtype\[B,(height/4)*(width/4)\] supplied standard normals.
#' @param nvae `logical`. Select miniature NVAE cell when true, default false.
#' @param height `integer`. Positive multiple-of-four output height, default28.
#' @param width `integer`. Positive multiple-of-four output width, default28.
#' @param channels `integer`. Positive feature width, default4.
#' @return `HierarchyPriorOutput`: Owning six-field ancestral prior output, including both supplied inputs.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(2609),state(0);
#' const auto out=n::models::HierarchyPrior(p,state,n::ones({2,2}),n::ones({2,49}));
#' // Caller supplies both latent noise groups; no posterior image is needed.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid inputs/geometry/stores or incompatible leaves.
#' @references - Vahdat and Kautz (2020), https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.03898 , top-down Gaussian hierarchy.
#' @seealso HierarchyPriorOutput, HierarchyDecode(), HierarchicalVariationalAutoencoder()
#' @md
#'
`HierarchyPrior` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1053`, NULL, list(...))
#' Evaluate a spatial two-group Gaussian hierarchy or its miniature residual-posterior NVAE variant.
#'
#' Two3x3 Swish convolutions and average pools encode an image at quarter resolution.
#' Dense Gaussian heads produce global top z2; its feature map gives learned p(z1|z2).
#' An image/top feature fusion gives direct lower posterior heads (ordinary hierarchy)
#' or residual mean/log-variance corrections (NVAE). Explicit noise columns first
#' drive the spatial lower group, then the top group: default49 lower +2 top=51.
#' BOTH sampled latent groups reach the convolutional decoder. No image/encoder skip
#' bypasses sampling. Ordinary Glorot weights and zero biases; NVAE BN gamma1/beta0.
#' Defaults have26 leaves/1977 scalars and no state, or37/2027 with six BN buffers/40
#' scalars for NVAE. Use separate stores per architecture and restore weights AND state.
#'
#' loss=mean(sum784 pixel NLL+(conditional lower KL+top KL)), preserving source
#' parenthesization. For non-default geometry use height*width pixels, Hq*Wq lower
#' coordinates and top_latent top coordinates. NVAE train_loss additionally adds
#' 1e-4*(sqrt(sum(prior_mu/W^2)+1e-12)+sqrt(sum(prior_lv/W^2)+1e-12)). Each prior
#' kernel is Cx1, so its spectral norm is its L2 norm before this smoothing. Only
#' these TWO kernels are penalized, not all NVAE kernels. No free bits, KL warmup,
#' variance clipping or posterior flows; this small adaptation is not full NVAE.
#'
#' NVAE training uses batch moments; update_stats=true also updates moving means
#' and unbiased variances ONCE in this forward, before the caller's optimizer step.
#' Extra gradient/inspection passes use training=true,update_stats=false. Inference
#' uses detached moving moments. State is a separate store that the caller excludes
#' from gradients/Adam. Initial calls lazily create missing weights/buffers. Existing
#' weights never change during a forward; existing state changes only with explicit
#' BN updates. Ordinary hierarchy ignores both training flags after validating them.
#'
#' Finite nonempty matching Float32/64 flat image and explicit noise matrices,
#' unit-range targets, safe multiples-of-four geometry and separate matching stores
#' are checked before leaf creation. Strides and all continuous image/noise/parameter
#' paths remain. Inputs unchanged; fields own lifetime. Incompatible leaves/computed
#' nonfinite values may fail later. No fitting, RNG or optimizer is hidden; synchronize
#' stores. Soft-target reconstruction is cross-entropy, binary targets give NLL.
#' @usage
#' HierarchicalVariationalAutoencoder(parameters, state, image, epsilon, nvae = FALSE, training = FALSE, update_stats = FALSE, height = 28, width = 28, channels = 4, top_latent = 2)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable trainable floating store; seed2609 reproduces this example.
#' @param state `ParameterStore`. Separate same-dtype BN moving-statistics store, seed0 in the example.
#' @param image `Tensor`. Finite unit-range\[B,height*width\] pixels, B>0.
#' @param epsilon `Tensor`. Finite same-dtype/batch\[B,Hq*Wq+top_latent\] explicit Gaussian noise.
#' @param nvae `logical`. Select residual posterior and miniature BN/SE cell, default false.
#' @param training `logical`. Use batch moments in NVAE when true, default false.
#' @param update_stats `logical`. Update existing NVAE statistics when true; requires training, default false.
#' @param height `integer`. Positive multiple-of-four image height, default28.
#' @param width `integer`. Positive multiple-of-four image width, default28.
#' @param channels `integer`. Positive encoder/top/decoder feature channels, default4.
#' @param top_latent `integer`. Positive global Gaussian dimension, default2.
#' @return `HierarchicalVaeOutput`: Owning nineteen-field forward with unregularized loss and separate train_loss.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(2609),state(0);
#' const auto out=n::models::HierarchicalVariationalAutoencoder(
#'     p,state,n::full({2,784},.2),n::zeros({2,51}),true,true,false);
#' const auto gradients=n::grad(out.train_loss,p.parameters());
#' // Caller updates weights and explicitly chooses which forward updates BN state.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid inputs/options/geometry/stores or incompatible leaves.
#' @references - Vahdat and Kautz (2020), NVAE: A Deep Hierarchical Variational Autoencoder,
#'   https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.03898 . Uses a two-group teaching adaptation.
#' - Sonderby et al. (2016), https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.02282 , Gaussian hierarchy;
#'   this model uses direct/additive residual heads rather than precision fusion.
#' @seealso HierarchicalVaeOutput, HierarchyPosterior(), ConditionalGaussianKl(), HierarchyNvaeCell(), HierarchyDecode(), HierarchyPrior()
#' @md
#'
`HierarchicalVariationalAutoencoder` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1054`, NULL, list(...))
#' Reduce sample-major log weights with a numerically shifted log-mean-exp.
#'
#' For finite nonempty Float32/64\[K,B\] input, returns log(sum_k exp(w\[k,b\]))-log(K).
#' The column maximum is gradient-stopped; all log-weight paths remain live,
#' including second derivatives. Float32 rounds log(K) to float before subtracting,
#' preserving the migrated calculation; Float64 uses full precision. K=1 returns
#' the input row exactly. Equal samples give the same value up to reduction rounding.
#' Finite extreme positive/negative weights avoid raw-exponential overflow, although
#' arithmetic overflow is not repaired. Strides are accepted. No RNG, mutation or
#' shared state; result owns lifetime. Work/storage O(K*B).
#' @usage
#' IwaeLogMeanExp(log_weights)
#' @param log_weights `Tensor`. Finite matching floating log weights\[K,B\], K,B positive.
#' @return `Tensor`: Owning differentiable per-case log mean importance weight\[B\], in nats.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' const auto bound=n::models::IwaeLogMeanExp(n::tensor({1000.,1001.,999.},{3,1}));
#' // Approximately1000.308994; raw exp(1000) would overflow Float64.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid shape/dtype or nonfinite weights.
#' @references - Burda, Grosse and Salakhutdinov (2015/2016), Importance Weighted Autoencoders,
#'   https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.00519 . Uses the multi-sample importance bound.
#' @seealso ImportanceWeightedBound(), ImportanceWeightedAutoencoder()
#' @md
#'
`IwaeLogMeanExp` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1055`, NULL, list(...))
#' Form the sampled Gaussian IWAE bound and effective sample size from explicit endpoints.
#'
#' The sample-major posterior endpoints are\[KB,L\]; pixel NLL is\[KB\]. Computes
#' logp=-.5*sum(z^2+log(2*pi)), logq=-.5*sum((z-mu)^2*exp(-lv)+lv+log(2*pi)),
#' logw=logp-NLL-logq, then reshapes\[K,B\] and reduces IwaeLogMeanExp. The source
#' parenthesization and Float32-rounded log(2*pi)/log(K) are preserved; Float64
#' uses full precision. This uses sampled densities, not analytic Gaussian KL.
#' Ordinary full pathwise gradients pass through mu,lv,z,NLL and all weights;
#' normalized weights are diagnostics, with no detachment or DReG replacement.
#'
#' Inputs must be finite same-dtype nonempty Float32/64 with matching Gaussian
#' shapes and rows divisible by samples. Finite signed NLL is accepted for general
#' log-density use. Strides accepted; inputs unchanged. No noise/optimizer/state.
#' All outputs own lifetime. Work/storage O(K*B*L); computed nonfinite values may
#' fail weight validation, and are not clipped or otherwise sanitized.
#' @usage
#' ImportanceWeightedBound(mu, log_variance, z, nll, samples)
#' @param mu `Tensor`. Finite posterior means\[KB,L\] in sample-major order.
#' @param log_variance `Tensor`. Finite matching posterior log variances\[KB,L\].
#' @param z `Tensor`. Explicit finite matching posterior samples\[KB,L\], retaining live paths.
#' @param nll `Tensor`. Finite same-dtype reconstruction negative log densities\[KB\].
#' @param samples `integer`. Positive K dividing KB exactly.
#' @return `ImportanceWeightedOutput`: Owning five-field objective/weight/ESS result; scalar mean is over cases.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' auto z=n::ones({6,2}).requires_grad_();
#' const auto out=n::models::ImportanceWeightedBound(n::zeros({6,2}),n::zeros({6,2}),
#'     z,n::full({6},3.),3);
#' const auto derivative=n::grad(out.loss,{z});
#' // q=p here, so sampled log-density terms cancel and every derivative is zero.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid shape/dtype/values/K or nonfinite computed log
#'   weights.
#' @references - Burda, Grosse and Salakhutdinov (2015/2016), Importance Weighted Autoencoders,
#'   https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.00519 . Uses ordinary reparameterized gradients.
#' @seealso ImportanceWeightedOutput, IwaeLogMeanExp(), GaussianReparameterize()
#' @md
#'
`ImportanceWeightedBound` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1056`, NULL, list(...))
#' Evaluate a multi-sample Gaussian/Bernoulli IWAE with explicit noise and ordinary gradients.
#'
#' Tiles image\[B,F\] K times sample-major, flattens epsilon\[K,B,L\] to\[KB,L\] and
#' calls BetaVariationalAutoencoder with beta=1, then ImportanceWeightedBound.
#' Encoding is repeated on the tiled matrix to preserve source GEMM rounding.
#' Only sampled latent codes enter the ReLU decoder; no image bypass exists.
#' Ten Glorot/zero-bias leaves use encoder,mu,log_variance,decoder,output names;
#' defaults F784,L4,H32 have51,416 scalars. GaussianVaeDecode reuses decoder leaves
#' for image-free prior decoding. K=1 is the sampled negative ELBO, not the
#' analytic-KL VAE estimator, although their expectations agree.
#'
#' Every parameter/image/noise path remains live. No fit, optimizer, checkpoint
#' selection, gradient estimator substitution or new random draws are hidden
#' beyond lazy initialization. Float32 matches source log constants; Float64
#' retains full precision. Validates finite pixels\[0,1\], noise/dtype/shape and
#' representable tiled/weight products before creating leaves. Incompatible
#' existing leaves and nonfinite computed posteriors may fail later. Strides are
#' accepted, inputs unchanged; all results own lifetime. Synchronize shared stores.
#' Work O(K*B*H*(F+L)); activation storage O(K*B*(F+H+L)).
#' @usage
#' ImportanceWeightedAutoencoder(parameters, image, epsilon, features = 784, latent = 4, hidden = 32)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable matching floating store; original seed2237.
#' @param image `Tensor`. Finite Float32/64 pixel matrix\[B,features\], B>0, values in\[0,1\].
#' @param epsilon `Tensor`. Finite matching explicit standard-normal noise\[K,B,latent\], K>0.
#' @param features `integer`. Positive pixel width F, default784.
#' @param latent `integer`. Positive code width L, default4.
#' @param hidden `integer`. Positive encoder/decoder ReLU width H, default32.
#' @return `ImportanceWeightedAutoencoderOutput`: Owning eleven-field output with ordinary fully pathwise sampled IWAE loss.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(2237);
#' const auto out=n::models::ImportanceWeightedAutoencoder(p,n::full({2,784},.2),
#'     n::ones({5,2,4}));
#' n::OptimizerConfig config;
#' config.kind=n::OptimizerKind::Adam;
#' n::Optimizer optimizer(p,.003,config);
#' optimizer.step(n::grad(out.loss,p.parameters()));
#' // One visible update; no optimization is hidden in the forward function.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid input/options/overflow or incompatible leaves.
#' @references - Burda, Grosse and Salakhutdinov (2015/2016), Importance Weighted Autoencoders,
#'   https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.00519 . Uses a small migrated educational architecture,
#'   not the paper's benchmark networks or a doubly reparameterized gradient estimator.
#' @seealso ImportanceWeightedAutoencoderOutput, ImportanceWeightedBound(), GaussianVaeDecode()
#' @md
#'
`ImportanceWeightedAutoencoder` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1057`, NULL, list(...))
#' Combine reconstruction, local Gaussian KL and signed aggregate MMD with explicit controls.
#'
#' Returns mean(nll+(1-alpha)*kl)+(alpha+lambda_weight-1)*mmd. NLL/KL are finite
#' matching nonempty Float32/64\[B\] vectors; mmd has one finite element of the same
#' dtype. Finite signed values, including negative unbiased MMD, are retained.
#' alpha in\[0,1\],lambda_weight>=1; Float32 narrows controls and computes both
#' coefficients in float before promotion, preserving source rounding. Float64
#' uses full precision. Positive alpha underflow and lambda overflow are rejected.
#' alpha1 removes local KL; alpha0/lambda1 gives mean(NLL+KL), though a full-model
#' call still computes its MMD diagnostics and requires at least two samples.
#' This generally is not an ordinary negative ELBO or a mutual-information estimate.
#' Inputs unchanged; owning scalar, all input paths stay live (zero weights give
#' zero derivatives). No fitting, RNG or shared state. Work O(B), no clipping.
#' @usage
#' InfoVaeObjective(nll, kl, mmd, alpha = 0.5, lambda_weight = 10.0)
#' @param nll `Tensor`. Finite reconstruction negative log densities\[B\], B>0.
#' @param kl `Tensor`. Finite matching local Gaussian KL\[B\].
#' @param mmd `Tensor`. Finite signed scalar/one-element aggregate MMD, same dtype.
#' @param alpha `numeric`. Finite coefficient in\[0,1\], default0.5.
#' @param lambda_weight `numeric`. Finite aggregate strength>=1, default10.
#' @return `Tensor`: Owning scalar InfoVAE objective with live reconstruction/KL/MMD paths.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' const auto
#' value=n::models::InfoVaeObjective(n::tensor({4.,6.}),n::tensor({2.,4.}),n::scalar(.25));
#' // mean(NLL)+.5*mean(KL)+9.5*MMD = 8.875.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid tensors, controls or unrepresentable options.
#' @references - Zhao, Song and Ermon (2017/2018), InfoVAE: Information Maximizing Variational
#'   Autoencoders, https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.02262 . Uses IMQ aggregate discrepancy.
#' @seealso InfoVariationalAutoencoder(), ImqMmd()
#' @md
#'
`InfoVaeObjective` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1058`, NULL, list(...))
#' Decode supplied codes through the InfoVAE tanh/linear decoder, without an image.
#'
#' Uses decoder/W\[L,H\],decoder/b\[H\],output/W\[H,F\],output/b\[F\] with ordinary Glorot
#' weights and zero biases; the full model shares these four leaves. This is the
#' tanh decoder, whereas GaussianVaeDecode uses ReLU. Finite signed/zero nonempty
#' matching Float32/64 codes retain code/parameter derivatives and accept strides.
#' Returns logits; apply sigmoid for probabilities, not Bernoulli pixel draws.
#' No encoder, targets, MMD, prior draws, fitting or optimizer is invoked. Prior
#' codes are caller-supplied standard-normal samples. Width/input checks precede
#' leaves; incompatible stored leaves may fail later. Computed overflow is not
#' repaired. Inputs unchanged; result owns lifetime. Synchronize shared stores.
#' Work O(B*H*(L+F)); activation storage O(B*(H+F)). Default decoder has13,408 scalars.
#' @usage
#' InfoVaeDecode(parameters, code, features = 784, latent = 4, hidden = 16)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable matching floating store containing/creating decoder leaves.
#' @param code `Tensor`. Finite code matrix\[B,latent\], B>0.
#' @param features `integer`. Positive pixel width, default784.
#' @param latent `integer`. Positive latent width, default4.
#' @param hidden `integer`. Positive tanh hidden width, default16.
#' @return `Tensor`: Owning differentiable logits\[B,features\].
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(3901);
#' const auto probability=n::sigmoid(n::models::InfoVaeDecode(p,n::zeros({20,4})));
#' // Twenty image-free prior-code probability images[20,784].
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid input/widths/overflow or incompatible leaves.
#' @references - Zhao, Song and Ermon (2017/2018), https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.02262 . Uses the
#'   migrated small tanh teaching architecture, not the paper's benchmark networks.
#' @seealso InfoVariationalAutoencoder(), GaussianVaeDecode()
#' @md
#'
`InfoVaeDecode` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1059`, NULL, list(...))
#' Evaluate an explicit-noise Gaussian InfoVAE/MMD-VAE with an independent supplied prior sample.
#'
#' Encoder F->H tanh feeds linear mean/log-variance heads\[L\]. Explicit epsilon
#' gives z=mu+exp(lv/2)*epsilon; only z enters InfoVaeDecode, no image bypass.
#' The aggregate discrepancy is ImqMmd(z,prior), not a discrepancy of means.
#' Loss is InfoVaeObjective; mean(NLL+KL) remains a separate diagnostic. Ten
#' ordinary Glorot/zero-bias leaves use encoder,mu,log_variance,decoder,output
#' prefixes in that creation order. Defaults F784,H16,L4 contain26,104 scalars.
#' alpha1/lambda10 gives MMD-VAE with zero local KL; alpha.5 retains.5KL+9.5MMD.
#' Objectives with different alpha are not directly comparable optimized ELBOs.
#'
#' Requires finite nonempty same-dtype Float32/64 image\[B,F\] in\[0,1\], explicit
#' epsilon\[B,L\] and independent caller-supplied prior\[P,L\], B,P>=2 (may differ).
#' All image/noise/prior/parameter paths, including second derivatives, stay live.
#' Prior changes affect MMD/loss but not posterior/decoder fields. Independence
#' is a statistical caller responsibility and cannot be inferred from arrays.
#' Strides accepted. Controls, inputs and pairwise/architecture products are
#' checked before leaves. Existing incompatible leaves or nonfinite computed
#' endpoints may fail later. No clipping of signed MMD, optimizer, fit, RNG draws
#' beyond lazy initialization or checkpoint selection is hidden. Inputs unchanged;
#' all fields own lifetime. Synchronize shared stores. Work/storage includes
#' O((B^2+P^2+B*P)*L) for kernels and O(B*H*(F+L)) for the Gaussian network.
#' @usage
#' InfoVariationalAutoencoder(parameters, image, epsilon, prior, alpha = 0.5, lambda_weight = 10.0, features = 784, latent = 4, hidden = 16)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable matching floating store; original seed3901.
#' @param image `Tensor`. Finite unit-range pixel rows\[B,features\], B>=2.
#' @param epsilon `Tensor`. Explicit finite matching Gaussian noise\[B,latent\].
#' @param prior `Tensor`. Explicit finite independent standard-normal samples\[P,latent\], P>=2.
#' @param alpha `numeric`. Finite local/aggregate control in\[0,1\], default0.5.
#' @param lambda_weight `numeric`. Finite aggregate control>=1, default10.
#' @param features `integer`. Positive pixel width, default784.
#' @param latent `integer`. Positive code width, default4.
#' @param hidden `integer`. Positive tanh hidden width, default16.
#' @return `InfoVaeOutput`: Owning sixteen-field output with signed MMD and separate diagnostic negative ELBO.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(3901);
#' const auto out=n::models::InfoVariationalAutoencoder(p,n::full({2,784},.2),
#'     n::ones({2,4}),n::zeros({3,4}));
#' const auto gradient=n::grad(out.loss,p.parameters());
#' // Caller owns Adam and checkpoint selection; all ten leaves retain gradients.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid inputs/options/products or incompatible leaves.
#' @references - Zhao, Song and Ermon (2017/2018), InfoVAE: Information Maximizing Variational
#'   Autoencoders, https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.02262 . Small three-scale IMQ teaching variant.
#' @seealso InfoVaeOutput, InfoVaeObjective(), ImqMmd(), InfoVaeDecode()
#' @md
#'
`InfoVariationalAutoencoder` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1060`, NULL, list(...))
#' Fuse bottom-up Gaussian evidence and a top-down Gaussian prior by adding precisions.
#'
#' For evidence N(em,exp(el)) and prior N(pm,exp(pl)), computes log precision
#' log(exp(-el)+exp(-pl)) using a detached maximum shift. Posterior log variance
#' is its negative; posterior mean is exp(post_lv-el)*em+exp(post_lv-pl)*pm.
#' The source parenthesization/rounding is retained. All four finite matching
#' nonempty Float32/64\[B,L\] inputs remain live, including second derivatives;
#' only the stabilizing maximum is detached. This is precision fusion, not
#' additive residual mean/log-variance heads. Identical input distributions halve
#' variance and retain mean up to rounding, rather than leaving variance unchanged.
#' Strides accepted, no broadcast between different shapes. No raw precision
#' exponential, RNG or input mutation. Outputs own lifetime, no shared state.
#' Work/storage O(B*L). Extreme-log cancellation and arithmetic overflow/underflow
#' are not repaired or clipped; a finite input does not guarantee accurate moments
#' at unrepresentably large log scales.
#' @usage
#' LadderPrecisionFusion(evidence_mu, evidence_lv, prior_mu, prior_lv)
#' @param evidence_mu `Tensor`. Finite evidence means\[B,L\].
#' @param evidence_lv `Tensor`. Finite matching evidence log variances\[B,L\].
#' @param prior_mu `Tensor`. Finite matching conditional prior means\[B,L\].
#' @param prior_lv `Tensor`. Finite matching conditional prior log variances\[B,L\].
#' @return `LadderGaussian`: Owning differentiable fused mean and log variance.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' const auto q=n::models::LadderPrecisionFusion(n::zeros({2,3}),n::zeros({2,3}),
#'     n::zeros({2,3}),n::zeros({2,3}));
#' // Fused mean0, log variance-log(2); two equal sources double precision.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid shapes/dtypes or nonfinite inputs.
#' @references - Sonderby, Raiko, Maaloe, Sonderby and Winther (2016), Ladder Variational
#'   Autoencoders, https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.02282 . Uses Gaussian precision fusion.
#' @seealso LadderGaussian, ConditionalGaussianKl(), HierarchyPosterior()
#' @md
#'
`LadderPrecisionFusion` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1061`, NULL, list(...))
#' Sample a Ladder lower prior from explicit top codes/noise and decode without an image.
#'
#' Applies prior_hidden ReLU, prior_mu/prior_lv linear heads, GaussianReparameterize
#' and sigmoid(GaussianVaeDecode(lower_z)). Uses the full model's same ten leaves;
#' defaults create26,216 scalars. There is no direct top-to-decoder bypass, encoder,
#' target, inference fusion, RNG draw beyond lazy initialization or hidden no-grad.
#' Finite matching nonempty Float32/64 top\[B,T\] and lower_noise\[B,L\] accept strides
#' and preserve both input/parameter paths. Zero lower noise gives the conditional
#' prior mean, not a marginal predictive mean. Caller supplies independent prior
#' draws and owns any no-grad guard, optimizer or checkpoints. Checks precede leaves;
#' incompatible existing leaves/nonfinite computed endpoints may fail later.
#' Inputs unchanged, all results own lifetime; synchronize shared stores. Work
#' O(B*(T*U+U*L+L*H+H*F)); storage includes those dense activations.
#' @usage
#' LadderPrior(parameters, top, lower_noise, features = 784, lower_latent = 4, top_latent = 2, hidden = 32, top_hidden = 16)
#' @param parameters `ParameterStore`. Mutable matching floating store.
#' @param top `Tensor`. Finite top prior samples\[B,top_latent\], B>0.
#' @param lower_noise `Tensor`. Finite explicit independent lower draws\[B,lower_latent\].
#' @param features `integer`. Positive pixel width, default784.
#' @param lower_latent `integer`. Positive lower Gaussian width, default4.
#' @param top_latent `integer`. Positive top Gaussian width, default2.
#' @param hidden `integer`. Positive decoder ReLU width, default32.
#' @param top_hidden `integer`. Positive conditional-prior ReLU width, default16.
#' @return `LadderPriorOutput`: Owning six-field ancestral prior output with differentiable probabilities.
#' @section Examples (C++):
#' ```cpp
#' #include <dm/dm.hpp>
#' namespace n=dm::nn;
#' n::ParameterStore p(2503);
#' const auto prior=n::models::LadderPrior(p,n::ones({20,2}),n::ones({20,4}));
#' const auto decoded=n::sigmoid(n::models::GaussianVaeDecode(p,prior.lower_z));
#' // decoded is exactly prior.probability; neither call uses an image.
#' ```
#' @section Errors:
#' Signals an R error (from the C++ exception):
#'
#' * `std::invalid_argument`: For invalid inputs/dimensions or incompatible leaves.
#' @references - Sonderby et al. (2016), Ladder Variational Autoencoders,
#'   https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.02282 . Uses two small Gaussian latent groups.
#' @seealso LadderPriorOutput, LadderVariationalAutoencoder(), GaussianVaeDecode()
#' @md
#'
`LadderPrior` <- function(...) .Call(`.rs_1062`, NULL, list(...))
#' Evaluate a two-level precision-fusion Ladder VAE with explicit top and lower noise.
#'
#' Image->hidden ReLU->top_hidden ReLU supplies lower evidence from the first
#' feature and top posterior heads from the second. Top noise samples z2; the
#' learned conditional p(z1|z2) combines with evidence via LadderPrecisionFusion.
#' Lower noise samples fused q(z1|x,z2); only z1 enters GaussianVaeDecode. The loss
#' is mean(pixel-summed NLL+(conditional lower KL+standard-normal top KL)), with
#' source reduction grouping. Conditional lower KL is sampled over top latents,
#' so inference should average all three terms over supplied top draws.
